# Master Textbook Structured Repository for all 38 Chapters
MASTER_TEXTBOOK_DATA = {('EM', 0): {'sub_id': 'EM', 'chap_idx': 0, 'title': 'Discrete Mathematics - Mathematical Logic & Set Theory', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Example 1: The Wi-Fi Login Password (What is a Proposition?)', 'text': "When you type your Wi-Fi password on your smartphone, the router tests only one question:\nIs the password Correct (True) or Incorrect (False)? There is no 'maybe' or 'half-correct'.\nIn computer science, any declarative statement that is strictly True or False is called a Proposition.\nSentences like 'What is your name?' (question) or 'Please pass the salt' (command) are NOT propositions because they cannot be assigned a true/false value."}, {'title': "Real-Life Example 2: Dad's Promise (How Implication P → Q Really Works)", 'text': "Imagine your father makes a promise: 'If you score AIR 1 in GATE (P), I will buy you a new bike (Q)'.\nWhen would you call your dad a liar?\n• Case 1: You get AIR 1 (T) and he buys the bike (T) ⇒ Promise kept! (T → T = True).\n• Case 2: You get AIR 1 (T) and he refuses to buy the bike (F) ⇒ Promise broken! (T → F = False).\n• Case 3: You did NOT get AIR 1 (F). Whether he buys the bike anyway or not, he never broke his original promise!\nThis is why whenever condition P is False, (P → Q) is ALWAYS considered mathematically TRUE. This is called Vacuous Truth."}, {'title': 'Real-Life Example 3: Social Media Friends (Relations Made Simple)', 'text': 'Think of people on Facebook or Instagram:\n• Reflexive: Can you see your own profile? Yes! Every person relates to themselves: (x, x) ∈ R.\n• Symmetric: On Facebook, if Alice is friends with Bob, Bob is automatically friends with Alice: (x, y) ∈ R implies (y, x) ∈ R.\n• Transitive: If Alice trusts Bob, and Bob trusts Charlie, does Alice automatically trust Charlie? If yes, it is Transitive.\nWhen a relation is Reflexive + Symmetric + Transitive, it divides everyone into clean friend circles called Equivalence Classes.'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '1.1', 'title': 'Propositions & Basic Logic Connectives', 'content': "A proposition is any statement that is strictly True (T) or False (F).<br/><br/>Here are the 5 core logical connectives explained in simple points:<br/>• <b>NOT (~P):</b> Flips the truth. If P is True, ~P is False.<br/>• <b>AND (P ∧ Q):</b> True ONLY if BOTH P and Q are True (like needing two security keys to open a locker).<br/>• <b>OR (P ∨ Q):</b> True if AT LEAST ONE of P or Q is True. False only when both are False.<br/>• <b>Implication (P → Q):</b> Means 'If P then Q'. It is logically equivalent to <b>(~P ∨ Q)</b>.<br/>• <b>Biconditional (P ↔ Q):</b> True when both P and Q have the exact same truth value (both True or both False).", 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: P → Q is False ONLY when P is True and Q is False. In all other 3 combinations, it is ALWAYS True!'}, {'number': '1.2', 'title': 'Contrapositive, Converse, and Inverse', 'content': "For any conditional statement 'If P then Q' (P → Q):<br/><br/>• <b>Contrapositive (~Q → ~P):</b> 'If NOT Q, then NOT P'.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>100% Logically Equivalent:</b> (P → Q) ≡ (~Q → ~P). Always use this when proving theorems in GATE!<br/><br/>• <b>Converse (Q → P):</b> 'If Q, then P'.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- NOT equivalent to the original statement.<br/><br/>• <b>Inverse (~P → ~Q):</b> 'If NOT P, then NOT Q'.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Equivalent to the Converse, but NOT the original statement.", 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: A statement and its Contrapositive always share the exact same truth table: (P → Q) ≡ (~Q → ~P).'}, {'number': '1.3', 'title': 'Quantifiers: For All (∀) vs There Exists (∃)', 'content': "• <b>Universal Quantifier (∀x):</b> Means 'For EVERY single item in the universe'.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If even one single item fails, the entire statement becomes False.<br/><br/>• <b>Existential Quantifier (∃x):</b> Means 'There exists AT LEAST ONE item'.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- As long as you can find one matching example, the statement is True.<br/><br/>• <b>De Morgan's Negation Rules for Quantifiers:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- ~[ ∀x P(x) ] &nbsp;≡&nbsp; ∃x [ ~P(x) ]. (Negating 'All birds fly' is 'There exists a bird that cannot fly').<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- ~[ ∃x P(x) ] &nbsp;≡&nbsp; ∀x [ ~P(x) ]. (Negating 'Someone scored 100' is 'Everyone scored not 100').", 'crucial_rule': "GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Negating 'All students passed' is NOT 'No students passed'. It is simply 'There is at least one student who failed'!"}, {'number': '1.4', 'title': 'Counting Relations on a Set of Size n', 'content': 'Let set A have n elements. The Cartesian product A × A has n² pairs.<br/><br/>Formulas to memorize for GATE:<br/>• Total possible relations = <b>2^(n²)</b>.<br/>• Total Reflexive relations = <b>2^(n² - n)</b>.<br/>• Total Symmetric relations = <b>2^(n(n+1)/2)</b>.<br/>• Total Reflexive and Symmetric relations = <b>2^(n(n-1)/2)</b>.<br/>• Total Equivalence relations = Counted by <b>Bell Numbers</b>:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;B(1) = 1, B(2) = 2, B(3) = 5, B(4) = 15, B(5) = 52.', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Remember Bell Number B(4) = 15. Questions asking for the number of equivalence relations on a set of 4 elements can be answered in 2 seconds with 15!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Counting Symmetric Relations', 'problem': 'Set A has 4 elements: A = {1, 2, 3, 4}. How many symmetric relations can be formed on set A?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Formula for symmetric relations on a set with n elements is: Total = 2^(n(n+1)/2).', 'Step 2: Here n = 4.', 'Step 3: Calculate the exponent: (4 × (4 + 1)) / 2 = (4 × 5) / 2 = 20 / 2 = 10.', 'Step 4: Compute 2^10 = 1024.'], 'answer': 'Total Symmetric Relations = 1024.'}], 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'GATE Trap: The Empty Relation (Vacuous Truth)', 'warning': 'Is the empty relation ∅ on a non-empty set symmetric? YES! It is vacuously symmetric and transitive, but it is NOT reflexive.'}]}, ('EM', 1): {'sub_id': 'EM', 'chap_idx': 1, 'title': 'Combinatorics, Recurrence Relations & Graph Theory', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Example 1: Sock Drawer in the Dark (Pigeonhole Principle)', 'text': 'You have a dark room with a drawer containing 10 Black socks and 10 White socks.\nHow many socks must you pull out to be 100% guaranteed to have at least one matching pair?\nPigeonholes (colors) = 2. Pigeons (socks pulled) = 3.\nBy Pigeonhole Principle, pulling 2 + 1 = 3 socks guarantees at least two socks share the same color!'}, {'title': 'Real-Life Example 2: Handshakes at a Party (Degree Sum Formula)', 'text': "Every time two people shake hands, exactly 2 hands are involved (one from each person).\nTherefore, if you count everyone's individual handshakes and add them all together, the grand total must be an EVEN number: Sum of Degrees = 2 × (Total Handshakes/Edges). It is impossible for an odd number of people to have shaken an odd number of hands!"}], 'foundations': [{'number': '2.1', 'title': 'Pigeonhole Principle (PHP) Formulas', 'content': '• <b>Basic PHP:</b> If n items (pigeons) are put into k boxes (pigeonholes) and n > k, at least one box contains ≥ 2 items.<br/><br/>• <b>Generalized PHP:</b> If n items are put into k boxes, at least one box must contain at least <b>⌈n / k⌉</b> items.<br/><br/>• <b>Minimum items needed for a guarantee:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;To guarantee that at least one box has m items, you need: <b>k(m - 1) + 1</b> items.', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In a group of n people, there are ALWAYS at least two people who have the exact same number of friends in that group!'}, {'number': '2.2', 'title': 'Graph Theory Core Theorems', 'content': "• <b>Handshaking Theorem:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Sum of degrees of all vertices = <b>2 × |E|</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Direct consequence: The number of vertices with odd degree is ALWAYS EVEN.<br/><br/>• <b>Euler's Formula for Planar Graphs:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;For any connected planar graph: <b>V - E + F = 2</b> (where F = faces/regions, including outer face).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Planar inequality: <b>E ≤ 3V - 6</b> (for V ≥ 3).<br/><br/>• <b>Bipartite Graph Test:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;A graph is bipartite if and only if it contains <b>NO ODD CYCLES</b> (cycles of length 3, 5, 7...).", 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Complete graph K_n has n(n-1)/2 edges. A tree with n vertices ALWAYS has exactly n - 1 edges and is 2-colorable!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Planar Graph Faces', 'problem': 'A connected planar graph has 10 vertices each of degree 3. How many faces/regions does it divide the plane into?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Use Handshaking Theorem to find edges: Sum of degrees = 10 × 3 = 30.', 'Step 2: 2E = 30 ⇒ E = 15 edges.', "Step 3: Apply Euler's Planar Formula: V - E + F = 2.", 'Step 4: Substitute V = 10, E = 15: 10 - 15 + F = 2 ⇒ -5 + F = 2 ⇒ F = 7.'], 'answer': 'Total Faces F = 7.'}], 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'GATE Trap: Euler Path vs Euler Circuit', 'warning': 'Euler Circuit (starts and ends at same vertex): Graph must be connected and ALL vertices have EVEN degree. Euler Path (open walk): Graph has EXACTLY TWO vertices with ODD degree.'}]}, ('EM', 2): {'sub_id': 'EM', 'chap_idx': 2, 'title': 'Linear Algebra - Matrices, Systems of Equations & Eigenvalues', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Example 1: Photo Zooming and Tilting (Eigenvectors & Transformations)', 'text': 'When you pinch-to-zoom a photo on Instagram, every pixel moves outward.\nMost pixels change both their position and their direction. But the straight diagonal lines shooting out from the center only stretch; their direction never bends!\nThose special directions that never change direction under transformation are called **Eigenvectors**, and the zoom scale factor is the **Eigenvalue** (λ).'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '3.1', 'title': 'System of Linear Equations (AX = B) Consistency', 'content': 'Let A be an m × n matrix, and [A | B] be the augmented matrix:<br/><br/>• <b>Unique Solution:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Rank(A) = Rank([A | B]) = n (number of variables).<br/><br/>• <b>Infinitely Many Solutions:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Rank(A) = Rank([A | B]) = r < n.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Number of free parameters / independent solutions = <b>n - r</b>.<br/><br/>• <b>No Solution (Inconsistent):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Rank(A) < Rank([A | B]).', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: For homogeneous system AX = 0: It ALWAYS has the trivial zero solution. It has non-trivial (infinite) solutions if and only if Determinant |A| = 0 (Rank < n)!'}, {'number': '3.2', 'title': 'Eigenvalue Properties (The 5 Golden Results)', 'content': '• <b>1. Sum of Eigenvalues:</b> Equal to the <b>Trace of Matrix A</b> (sum of main diagonal elements):<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;λ1 + λ2 + ... + λn = Trace(A).<br/><br/>• <b>2. Product of Eigenvalues:</b> Equal to the <b>Determinant |A|</b>:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;λ1 × λ2 × ... × λn = det(A).<br/><br/>• <b>3. Powers of A:</b> The eigenvalues of A^k are <b>λ^k</b>.<br/><br/>• <b>4. Inverse:</b> If A is invertible, eigenvalues of A^(-1) are <b>1 / λ</b>.<br/><br/>• <b>5. Triangular/Diagonal Matrices:</b> The eigenvalues are simply the <b>main diagonal entries</b>!', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In 90% of GATE questions on eigenvalues, you do NOT need to solve the characteristic equation det(A - λI) = 0! Just check which option matches Trace and Determinant!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Finding Eigenvalues Quickly', 'problem': 'Matrix A is [[4, 2], [2, 1]]. Find its eigenvalues without solving quadratic equations.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Find Trace(A) = Main diagonal sum = 4 + 1 = 5.', 'Step 2: Find Determinant(A) = (4 × 1) - (2 × 2) = 4 - 4 = 0.', 'Step 3: We need two numbers λ1, λ2 such that λ1 + λ2 = 5 and λ1 × λ2 = 0.', 'Step 4: Clearly, one eigenvalue is 0 and the other is 5.'], 'answer': 'Eigenvalues are λ = 0 and λ = 5.'}], 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'GATE Trap: Zero Eigenvalue', 'warning': 'If a matrix has λ = 0 as an eigenvalue, its determinant is 0, which means the matrix is SINGULAR and has NO inverse!'}]}, ('EM', 3): {'sub_id': 'EM', 'chap_idx': 3, 'title': "Calculus, Probability Distributions & Bayes' Theorem", 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Example 1: Speedometer vs Odometer (Derivative vs Integral)', 'text': '• Derivative: Your speedometer needle showing 60 km/h right at this exact split-second (instantaneous rate of change).\n• Integral: Your trip meter showing total kilometers accumulated over the whole journey (area under the speed curve).'}, {'title': "Real-Life Example 2: Medical Test Result (Bayes' Theorem)", 'text': "A rare disease affects 1 in 1000 people. A lab test is 99% accurate.\nIf you test positive, is there a 99% chance you are sick? Surprisingly NO! Because the disease is so rare, false positives from healthy people outnumber true positives from sick people. Bayes' Theorem updates probability using real prior base rates."}], 'foundations': [{'number': '4.1', 'title': "L'Hôpital's Rule & Maxima-Minima", 'content': "• <b>L'Hôpital's Rule:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;If limit gives indeterminate form <code>0 / 0</code> or <code>∞ / ∞</code>:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>lim [f(x) / g(x)] = lim [f'(x) / g'(x)]</code> (differentiate numerator and denominator separately).<br/><br/>• <b>Finding Local Maxima and Minima:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Set first derivative to zero: <code>f'(x) = 0</code> to find stationary/critical points.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. Compute second derivative <code>f''(x)</code>:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- If <code>f''(x) < 0</code> ⇒ <b>Local Maximum</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- If <code>f''(x) > 0</code> ⇒ <b>Local Minimum</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- If <code>f''(x) == 0</code> ⇒ Test fails (check higher derivatives).", 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Always check the boundary endpoints of a closed interval [a, b] when finding the Global/Absolute maximum or minimum!'}, {'number': '4.2', 'title': "Bayes' Theorem & Conditional Probability", 'content': "• <b>Conditional Probability:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>P(A | B) = P(A ∩ B) / P(B)</code>.<br/><br/>• <b>Independent Events:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;A and B are independent if and only if <code>P(A ∩ B) = P(A) × P(B)</code>.<br/><br/>• <b>Bayes' Formula:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>P(A_i | B) = [ P(B | A_i) × P(A_i) ] / [ Σ P(B | A_j) × P(A_j) ]</code>.", 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Mutually exclusive events with non-zero probability are NEVER independent! If P(A ∩ B) = 0, then P(A) × P(B) cannot equal 0 unless one has probability 0.'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Evaluating Indeterminate Limit', 'problem': 'Evaluate limit as x → 0 of (sin 3x) / x.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Check form by substituting x = 0: sin(0) / 0 = 0 / 0 (Indeterminate form).', "Step 2: Apply L'Hôpital's Rule: differentiate numerator and denominator with respect to x.", 'Step 3: d/dx [sin 3x] = 3 cos 3x. d/dx [x] = 1.', 'Step 4: New limit = lim [3 cos 3x / 1] as x → 0 = 3 cos(0) = 3 × 1 = 3.'], 'answer': 'Limit = 3.'}], 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'GATE Trap: Variance Formula', 'warning': "Variance Var(X) = E[X^2] - (E[X])^2. Also remember: Var(aX + b) = a^2 × Var(X). Adding a constant 'b' never changes the spread/variance!"}]}, ('DL', 0): {'sub_id': 'DL', 'chap_idx': 0, 'title': 'Number Systems, Signed Numbers & IEEE-754 Floating Point', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': "Real-Life Example 1: Car Odometer Overflow (Why 2's Complement is Brilliant)", 'text': "When an odometer reading 999999 clicks one more mile, it rolls over to 000000.\nIn computer arithmetic, 2's complement uses this exact rollover trick: subtracting a number is identical to adding its complement!\nBecause of this, digital processors don't need a separate subtraction circuit. The exact same binary adder performs both addition and subtraction."}], 'foundations': [{'number': '5.1', 'title': 'Signed Number Ranges for n bits', 'content': "For an n-bit binary representation:<br/><br/>• <b>Unsigned:</b> 0 to <b>2^n - 1</b>.<br/>• <b>Sign-Magnitude:</b> -(2^(n-1) - 1) to +(2^(n-1) - 1). (Has two zeros: +0 and -0).<br/>• <b>1's Complement:</b> -(2^(n-1) - 1) to +(2^(n-1) - 1). (Has two zeros).<br/>• <b>2's Complement:</b> <b>-2^(n-1)</b> to <b>+(2^(n-1) - 1)</b>. (Only ONE unique zero!).", 'crucial_rule': "GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In 2's complement with n = 8 bits: Range is -128 to +127. Notice that -128 has NO positive counterpart (+128 cannot fit in 8 signed bits)!"}, {'number': '5.2', 'title': 'IEEE-754 Single Precision (32-bit) Floating Point', 'content': 'The 32 bits are split into 3 fields:<br/>• <b>Sign bit (S):</b> 1 bit (0 for positive, 1 for negative).<br/>• <b>Exponent (E):</b> 8 bits with <b>Bias = 127</b>. Actual exponent <code>e = E - 127</code>.<br/>• <b>Mantissa (M):</b> 23 bits representing fractional part with implicit leading 1: <code>1.M</code>.<br/><br/>• <b>Formula:</b> <code>Value = (-1)^S × (1.M) × 2^(E - 127)</code>.', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: If E = 255 and M = 0: Represents ±Infinity. If E = 255 and M ≠ 0: Represents NaN (Not a Number). If E = 0 and M = 0: Represents 0.'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': "Step-by-Step Solved Problem: 2's Complement Negation", 'problem': "Find the 8-bit 2's complement representation of -29.", 'steps': ['Step 1: Write +29 in 8-bit binary: 29 = 16 + 8 + 4 + 1 = 00011101.', "Step 2: Invert all bits (1's complement): 11100010.", 'Step 3: Add 1: 11100010 + 1 = 11100011.', 'Step 4: Verify: -128 + 64 + 32 + 2 + 1 = -128 + 99 = -29.'], 'answer': "-29 in 8-bit 2's complement = 11100011."}], 'exam_traps': [{'title': "GATE Trap: Detecting Overflow in 2's Complement", 'warning': 'Overflow occurs when: (1) Adding two positive numbers yields a negative result, OR (2) Adding two negative numbers yields a positive result. In hardware: Overflow = Carry_In_to_MSB ⊕ Carry_Out_from_MSB!'}]}, ('DL', 1): {'sub_id': 'DL', 'chap_idx': 1, 'title': 'Boolean Algebra, Logic Gates & K-Map Minimization', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Example 1: Hallway Two-Way Light Switches (XOR Gate)', 'text': 'You have a stair light with one switch downstairs (A) and one upstairs (B).\nFlipping EITHER switch toggles the light. If both switches are in the same position (both up or both down), the light is OFF. If they are in different positions, the light is ON.\nThis behavior is the textbook definition of an **XOR Gate**: output is 1 when inputs differ.'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '6.1', 'title': 'Universal Gates & Consensus Theorem', 'content': '• <b>Universal Gates:</b> <b>NAND</b> and <b>NOR</b> can build any logic circuit without any other gate.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Minimum NAND gates to make NOT = 1, AND = 2, OR = 3, XOR = 4, XNOR = 5.<br/><br/>• <b>Consensus Theorem:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>AB + ~A C + BC = AB + ~A C</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;(The redundant term <code>BC</code> can be eliminated entirely!).', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: To make XOR gate using only NAND gates takes 4 gates. To make XOR using only NOR gates takes 5 gates!'}, {'number': '6.2', 'title': 'K-Map Rules & Essential Prime Implicants', 'content': "• <b>Implicant:</b> Any group of 1s in a K-map.<br/>• <b>Prime Implicant (PI):</b> A rectangle of size 1, 2, 4, 8, 16 that cannot be merged into a larger group.<br/>• <b>Essential Prime Implicant (EPI):</b> A Prime Implicant that covers at least one '1' that is NOT covered by any other PI.<br/>• <b>Minimal SOP expression:</b> Must include ALL Essential Prime Implicants!", 'crucial_rule': "GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Don't-care conditions (X) can be set to 1 if they help enlarge a group, or set to 0 if they don't. You never need to cover an isolated don't-care!"}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Consensus Theorem Simplification', 'problem': "Simplify the Boolean function: F = A B + A' C + B C.", 'steps': ["Step 1: Notice terms: A B has variable A, A' C has inverted variable A'.", 'Step 2: The remaining variables from both terms are B and C, forming the third term B C.', 'Step 3: By Consensus Theorem, B C is completely redundant.', "Step 4: Simplified expression = A B + A' C."], 'answer': "Minimal F = A B + A' C."}], 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'GATE Trap: Gray Code in K-Maps', 'warning': 'K-Map rows and columns follow Gray Code: 00, 01, 11, 10. Notice 11 comes before 10 so that only 1 bit flips between adjacent cells!'}]}, ('DL', 2): {'sub_id': 'DL', 'chap_idx': 2, 'title': 'Combinational Circuits - Multiplexers, Decoders & Adders', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Example 1: TV Remote Channel Selector (Multiplexer - MUX)', 'text': "A cable dish receives 64 TV channels at once. Your TV screen can only display 1 channel at a time.\nWhen you punch channel number '4' on your remote control (Select lines), the TV switches and displays only channel 4. This is a Multiplexer: Many inputs in, 1 output out."}], 'foundations': [{'number': '7.1', 'title': 'Multiplexer (MUX) as a Universal Circuit', 'content': '• A 2^n to 1 MUX has 2^n data inputs, n select lines, and 1 output.<br/>• <b>Implementing any n-variable function:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Using a 2^n to 1 MUX: Needs <b>NO external gates</b>! Connect variables to select lines.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Using a 2^(n-1) to 1 MUX: Connect (n-1) variables to select lines, and the remaining variable (0, 1, x, ~x) to data inputs.', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Any Boolean function of n variables can be implemented using a single 2^(n-1) to 1 MUX with at most an inverter for the inputs!'}, {'number': '7.2', 'title': 'Half Adder vs Full Adder', 'content': '• <b>Half Adder (Adds 2 bits A, B):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Sum = A ⊕ B.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Carry = A · B.<br/><br/>• <b>Full Adder (Adds 3 bits A, B, C_in):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Sum = A ⊕ B ⊕ C_in.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Carry = A·B + B·C_in + A·C_in = A·B + C_in·(A ⊕ B).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- A Full Adder can be built from: <b>2 Half Adders and 1 OR gate</b>.', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Carry Lookahead Adder (CLA) eliminates the slow ripple delay of standard adders. Generation bit G_i = A_i · B_i; Propagation bit P_i = A_i ⊕ B_i. CLA calculates all carries in parallel in O(1) time!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Building Larger MUX from Smaller MUX', 'problem': 'How many 2-to-1 Multiplexers are needed to build a 16-to-1 Multiplexer?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Level 1: 16 inputs need 16 / 2 = 8 MUXes (produces 8 outputs).', 'Step 2: Level 2: 8 inputs need 8 / 2 = 4 MUXes (produces 4 outputs).', 'Step 3: Level 3: 4 inputs need 4 / 2 = 2 MUXes (produces 2 outputs).', 'Step 4: Level 4: 2 inputs need 2 / 2 = 1 MUX (produces 1 final output).', 'Step 5: Total MUXes = 8 + 4 + 2 + 1 = 15.'], 'answer': 'Total 2-to-1 MUXes needed = 15.'}], 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'GATE Trap: Active-Low Decoders', 'warning': 'Many exam questions use decoders with active-low outputs (outputs are 0 when active, 1 when inactive). For active-low outputs, MINTERMS require NAND gates instead of OR gates!'}]}, ('DL', 3): {'sub_id': 'DL', 'chap_idx': 3, 'title': 'Sequential Circuits, Flip-Flops & Counters', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Example 1: Elevator Floor Counter (Sequential State Memory)', 'text': 'An elevator cannot decide whether to go UP or DOWN just by looking at what button you pressed.\nIt MUST remember its Current State: What floor is it on right now? (Are we at floor 2 or floor 7?).\nA combinational circuit has no memory. A sequential circuit uses Flip-Flops as memory cells to store the current state.'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '8.1', 'title': 'Flip-Flop Characteristic Equations', 'content': "• <b>SR Flip-Flop:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>Q_next = S + R' Q</code> &nbsp;(Constraint: S·R = 0, input S=1, R=1 is FORBIDDEN).<br/><br/>• <b>JK Flip-Flop (Eliminates forbidden state):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>Q_next = J Q' + K' Q</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- J=1, K=1 causes <b>Toggle</b> (Q flips to Q').<br/><br/>• <b>D Flip-Flop (Data delay):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>Q_next = D</code>.<br/><br/>• <b>T Flip-Flop (Toggle):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>Q_next = T ⊕ Q</code>.", 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Race-Around condition occurs in Level-Triggered JK flip-flops when J=1, K=1 and clock pulse width t_p > propagation delay t_pd. Solved by: Edge-Triggered Flip-Flops or Master-Slave JK Flip-Flops!'}, {'number': '8.2', 'title': 'Counters: Modulus & States', 'content': "• <b>MOD-N Counter:</b> Counts N distinct states from 0 to N - 1. Requires <b>k = ⌈log2 N⌉</b> flip-flops.<br/><br/>• <b>Ring Counter (n flip-flops):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Exactly one '1' rotates: 1000 → 0100 → 0010 → 0001.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Total distinct states = <b>n</b>.<br/><br/>• <b>Johnson / Twisted-Ring Counter (n flip-flops):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Inverted output Q' feeds back to input D.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Total distinct states = <b>2n</b>.", 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: With 4 flip-flops: A normal counter has 2^4 = 16 states. A Johnson counter has 2 × 4 = 8 states. A Ring counter has only 4 states!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Johnson Counter Sequence', 'problem': 'A 3-bit Johnson counter starts in state 000. Write its entire state transition cycle.', 'steps': ["Step 1: Rule: In a Johnson counter, Q2 Q1 Q0 moves right, and Q0' enters at Q2.", "Step 2: Start: 000 ⇒ Q0 = 0, so Q0' = 1 enters left ⇒ Next: 100.", 'Step 3: From 100 ⇒ Q0 = 0, so 1 enters left ⇒ Next: 110.', 'Step 4: From 110 ⇒ Q0 = 0, so 1 enters left ⇒ Next: 111.', "Step 5: From 111 ⇒ Q0 = 1, so Q0' = 0 enters left ⇒ Next: 011.", 'Step 6: From 011 ⇒ Q0 = 1, so 0 enters left ⇒ Next: 001.', 'Step 7: From 001 ⇒ Q0 = 1, so 0 enters left ⇒ Next: 000 (Cycle complete!).'], 'answer': 'Cycle of 6 states (2n = 2×3 = 6): 000 → 100 → 110 → 111 → 011 → 001 → 000.'}], 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'GATE Trap: Asynchronous Counter Clock Delay', 'warning': 'In an Asynchronous (Ripple) counter of n flip-flops, each flip-flop is clocked by the previous one. Total ripple delay = n × t_pd. Maximum clock frequency f_max = 1 / (n × t_pd)!'}]}, ('COA', 0): {'sub_id': 'COA', 'chap_idx': 0, 'title': 'Machine Instructions & Addressing Modes', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Example 1: The Scavenger Hunt Clues (Addressing Modes Made Simple)', 'text': "Imagine you are playing a scavenger hunt game to find cash prize money:\n• Immediate Mode: The clue paper has the cash ₹500 taped directly onto it! You get the money instantly without going anywhere.\n• Direct Mode: The paper gives you an address: 'Go to House #42'. You walk there and pick up the money from the house.\n• Indirect Mode: The paper says: 'Open locker 10; inside locker 10 is a key and another note with the final address'. You make two trips.\n• Register Mode: The money is already sitting inside your own pocket (CPU register). Zero walking required!\n• PC-Relative Mode: The paper says: 'From where you are standing right now, take 5 steps forward'. This is how loops and jumps work in software."}, {'title': 'Real-Life Example 2: Little-Endian vs Big-Endian (How Bytes are Stored)', 'text': 'When writing a mailing address, which order do you use?\n• Big-Endian: You write the biggest part first (Country, then State, then City, then House number). The Most Significant Byte (MSB) gets stored at the lowest memory address.\n• Little-Endian: You write the smallest part first (House number first, then City, State, and Country last). The Least Significant Byte (LSB) gets stored at the lowest memory address.\n• Everyday fact: Intel x86 processors use Little-Endian. Internet network packets use Big-Endian.'}, {'title': 'Real-Life Example 3: Expanding Menu Combos (Expanding Opcode Technique)', 'text': "In a burger shop, you have a 4-bit order slip that can represent 16 total combo meals. But what if you need some large meals (that take 3 extra side-dishes) and some small meals (that only need 2 side-dishes)?\nBy keeping 14 codes for large meals and reserving the remaining 2 unused codes as 'escape signals', you can expand the order slip to unlock dozens of smaller meal options. This is called Expanding Opcode."}], 'foundations': [{'number': '9.1', 'title': 'Effective Address (EA) in Common Addressing Modes', 'content': '<b>What is Effective Address (EA)?</b><br/>It is the exact physical RAM location where your data (operand) lives.<br/><br/>Here is how each addressing mode finds the data, explained point by point:<br/>• <b>Immediate Addressing:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- The data is written directly inside the instruction itself (e.g. <code>ADD R1, #5</code>).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- EA = None (No RAM lookup needed). Number of memory accesses to fetch data = 0.<br/>• <b>Direct Addressing:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- The instruction directly gives the RAM address (e.g. <code>LOAD R1, [1000]</code>).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- EA = Address field. Memory accesses = 1 (to read data from address 1000).<br/>• <b>Indirect Addressing:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- The instruction points to an address, which holds ANOTHER address where data is stored.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- EA = Content of Memory[Address]. Memory accesses = 2 (first to read the pointer, second to read the data).<br/>• <b>Register Addressing:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- The data is stored inside a fast CPU register (e.g. <code>ADD R1, R2</code>).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- EA = None. Memory accesses = 0 (blazing fast!).<br/>• <b>Register Indirect Addressing:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- A register holds the RAM memory address of the data (like a C pointer).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- EA = Content of Register. Memory accesses = 1.<br/>• <b>PC-Relative Addressing:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Used for jump and branch instructions (<code>if/else</code>, <code>loops</code>).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- EA = Next Instruction PC + Offset.', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In PC-Relative mode, the Program Counter (PC) has ALREADY moved to the next instruction before the offset is added! Formula: Target Address = Next_PC + Offset.'}, {'number': '9.2', 'title': 'Hardwired vs Microprogrammed Control Unit', 'content': '<b>What does a Control Unit do?</b><br/>It acts like the conductor of an orchestra, generating electronic signals that tell the ALU, registers, and memory when to read, write, or calculate.<br/><br/>Two ways to build it:<br/>• <b>Hardwired Control Unit:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Built using physical logic gates (AND, OR, flip-flops).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Speed: Extremely fast (used in RISC processors).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Flexibility: Zero flexibility. If you find a bug, you cannot change the hardware after manufacturing.<br/>• <b>Microprogrammed Control Unit:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Control signals are written as tiny binary micro-instructions stored in a special ROM called Control Memory.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Speed: Slower (because it must fetch micro-instructions from ROM).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Flexibility: Very flexible and easy to update via firmware (used in CISC processors).<br/>• <b>Horizontal vs Vertical Microprogramming:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Horizontal: 1 bit per control signal. No decoder needed. Micro-instructions are very wide, but super fast.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Vertical: Signals are encoded in groups and decoded using decoders (e.g. 3 bits decoded to 8 signals). Words are narrower, saving memory.', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: If a control unit has 32 independent control signals: Horizontal microprogramming needs 32 bits. Vertical microprogramming with 4 groups of 8 mutually exclusive signals needs only 4 × log2(8) = 12 bits!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Expanding Opcode', 'problem': 'A 16-bit computer uses 4-bit register addresses. It supports 3-register instructions and 2-register instructions. If there are 14 different 3-register instructions, how many 2-register instructions can be supported?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Check total instruction length = 16 bits.', 'Step 2: A 3-register instruction needs: Opcode + Reg1(4 bits) + Reg2(4 bits) + Reg3(4 bits) = Opcode + 12 bits.', 'Step 3: Bits available for Opcode = 16 - 12 = 4 bits. Total possible opcode combinations = 2^4 = 16.', 'Step 4: The system uses 14 combinations for 3-register instructions. Unused opcode patterns = 16 - 14 = 2 patterns.', 'Step 5: For a 2-register instruction, only 2 × 4 = 8 bits are used for registers. The opcode can expand by 4 extra bits!', 'Step 6: Total 2-register instructions possible = (Unused patterns) × 2^(extra bits) = 2 × 2^4 = 2 × 16 = 32.'], 'answer': 'Maximum 2-register instructions supported = 32.'}], 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'GATE Trap: Byte-Addressable vs Word-Addressable Memory', 'warning': 'Always read the question carefully: Is the memory Byte-addressable (1 byte per address) or Word-addressable (e.g. 4 bytes per address)? For a 64 MB memory: If byte-addressed, you need 26 bits (since 64 MB = 2^26 B). If 4-byte-word addressed, you need only 24 bits (since 64 MB / 4 B = 16 M words = 2^24 words)!'}]}, ('COA', 1): {'sub_id': 'COA', 'chap_idx': 1, 'title': 'ALU, Control Unit & Memory Interfacing', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': "Real-Life Example 1: Multiplying by 99 in Your Head (Booth's Algorithm)", 'text': "If someone asks you to multiply 27 × 99, do you perform 99 separate additions? Of course not!\nYou simply do 27 × (100 - 1) = 2700 - 27 = 2673. That took just one subtraction and one shift!\nBooth's Algorithm does the exact same trick in binary: when it sees a long string of 1s (like 0111110), it does 1 subtraction at the beginning and 1 addition at the end, skipping all the steps in between."}, {'title': 'Real-Life Example 2: The VIP Express Highway (Direct Memory Access - DMA)', 'text': 'Imagine downloading a 4K movie from your SSD into RAM. If the CPU had to move every single byte by hand, your entire computer would freeze.\nInstead, a DMA Controller acts like an express highway: it takes control of the memory bus and streams data straight into RAM while the CPU keeps running your games without waiting.'}, {'title': 'Real-Life Example 3: Phone Notification vs Continuous Screen Checking (Interrupts vs Polling)', 'text': '• Polling (Programmed I/O): Checking your phone screen every 5 seconds to see if a WhatsApp message arrived. Wastes huge battery and CPU time!\n• Interrupt-Driven I/O: You keep working on your laptop. When a message arrives, your phone chimes (sends an interrupt signal), you quickly read it, and return to work.'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '10.1', 'title': "Booth's Multiplication Rules (Q_i, Q_{i-1})", 'content': "<b>How Booth's Algorithm works:</b><br/>• It multiplies signed numbers in 2's complement without extra sign conversion.<br/>• You scan multiplier bits from right to left, starting with an extra helper bit Q_{-1} = 0.<br/><br/><b>The 4 Simple Action Rules:</b><br/>• <b>Case (0, 0):</b> String of zeros. Action: Only Arithmetic Right Shift (ARS).<br/>• <b>Case (0, 1):</b> End of a string of 1s. Action: Add Multiplicand (A = A + M), then ARS.<br/>• <b>Case (1, 0):</b> Beginning of a string of 1s. Action: Subtract Multiplicand (A = A - M), then ARS.<br/>• <b>Case (1, 1):</b> Inside a string of 1s. Action: Only Arithmetic Right Shift (ARS).", 'crucial_rule': "GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In Booth's algorithm, the shift is strictly an ARITHMETIC Right Shift (ARS). The sign bit (MSB) is copied into itself so the sign of positive and negative numbers is always preserved!"}, {'number': '10.2', 'title': 'DMA Cycle Stealing Percentage', 'content': '<b>What is Cycle Stealing?</b><br/>The DMA controller transfers one data word, pauses, gives the bus back to the CPU, and repeats.<br/><br/><b>Formulas to remember:</b><br/>• Time taken by DMA to transfer 1 word = (1 Word Size) / (Device Transfer Rate).<br/>• Fraction of CPU time stolen = (Time bus is busy with DMA) / (Total Time).<br/>• <b>Direct Shortcut:</b> CPU Time Stolen (%) = (Device Transfer Rate / Memory Bus Bandwidth) × 100%.', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Percentage of CPU time available for useful processing = 100% - (Percentage stolen by DMA)!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Step-by-Step Solved Problem: DMA Bus Overhead', 'problem': 'A hard disk transfers data at 2 MB/sec. The memory bus has cycle time 200 ns and transfers 1 byte per cycle. In cycle-stealing mode, what percentage of CPU time is stolen by DMA?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Find Memory Bus Bandwidth = 1 Byte / (200 × 10^-9 sec) = 5,000,000 Bytes/sec = 5 MB/sec.', 'Step 2: Hard disk transfer rate = 2 MB/sec.', 'Step 3: Calculate the ratio of bus usage = (Device Rate) / (Bus Bandwidth) = 2 MB/s / 5 MB/s = 0.40.', 'Step 4: Multiply by 100% to find percentage = 40%.'], 'answer': 'CPU time consumed by DMA = 40% (CPU gets remaining 60% of time).'}], 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'GATE Trap: Vectored vs Non-Vectored Interrupts', 'warning': 'Vectored interrupt: The interrupting device supplies the handler address directly on the data bus. Non-vectored interrupt: The CPU jumps to a fixed, hardwired memory address in the interrupt vector table.'}]}, ('COA', 2): {'sub_id': 'COA', 'chap_idx': 2, 'title': 'Memory Hierarchy, Cache Mapping & AMAT', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Example 1: The Kitchen Spice Rack (Why Cache Works)', 'text': "When cooking, a chef keeps salt, pepper, and oil on a tiny shelf right above the stove (L1 Cache: 1-second access).\nThey don't walk down to the basement grocery store (Main Memory: 2-minute walk) every time they need a pinch of salt!\nBecause you reuse the same ingredients (Temporal Locality) and nearby ingredients (Spatial Locality), 95% of cooking needs are met straight from the spice shelf."}, {'title': 'Real-Life Example 2: Assigned Parking Spots (Direct Mapped Cache)', 'text': 'In an apartment parking lot, car owner #14 is strictly assigned to Spot #4 (14 mod 10 = 4).\nIt is super fast to check if their car is home: you only check Spot #4! But if resident #24 arrives, they both fight for Spot #4 even if spots 5, 6, 7 are completely empty. This is Conflict Miss.'}, {'title': 'Real-Life Example 3: Free-For-All Parking (Fully Associative Cache)', 'text': 'Any car can park in any empty spot. Zero conflict misses! But when you search for your car, you must scan all 100 spots at the exact same time using expensive comparator hardware.'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '11.1', 'title': 'Breaking Down a Memory Address in Cache Mapping', 'content': 'Every memory address is split into 3 fields depending on cache mapping:<br/><br/>• <b>1. Direct Mapped Cache:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- [ Tag Bits | Line/Block Bits | Word Offset Bits ]<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Word Offset = log2(Block Size in bytes).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Line Bits = log2(Number of Cache Lines).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Tag Bits = Total Address Bits - (Line Bits + Word Offset).<br/><br/>• <b>2. k-Way Set Associative Cache:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- [ Tag Bits | Set Bits | Word Offset Bits ]<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Number of Sets = (Total Cache Lines) / k.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Set Bits = log2(Number of Sets).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Tag Bits = Total Address Bits - (Set Bits + Word Offset).<br/><br/>• <b>3. Fully Associative Cache:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- [ Tag Bits | Word Offset Bits ]<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- There is no set or line index. Tag Bits = Total Address Bits - Word Offset.', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Total Cache Size = (Number of Lines) × (Block Size). Tag Directory Size = (Number of Lines) × (Tag Bits + Valid Bit + Dirty Bit).'}, {'number': '11.2', 'title': 'Average Memory Access Time (AMAT)', 'content': '<b>Formula for AMAT:</b><br/>• <b>Hierarchical Cache (CPU checks L1 first; on miss, visits L2/Main Memory):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;AMAT = Hit_Time_L1 + (Miss_Rate_L1 × Miss_Penalty_L1).<br/><br/>• <b>Simultaneous Access (CPU searches L1 and Main Memory simultaneously):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;AMAT = (Hit_Rate × Hit_Time) + ((1 - Hit_Rate) × Main_Memory_Access_Time).', 'crucial_rule': "GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Unless the question explicitly specifies 'simultaneous access', ALWAYS use the Hierarchical formula where Miss Penalty includes the access to the next lower level!"}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Cache Tag Size Calculation', 'problem': 'A 32-bit byte-addressable system has a 64 KB 4-way set associative cache with 32-byte blocks. Find the number of Tag bits.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Find Word Offset: Block size = 32 Bytes = 2^5 B ⇒ Offset = 5 bits.', 'Step 2: Find Total Cache Lines: Total Cache Size / Block Size = 64 KB / 32 B = 65536 / 32 = 2048 lines.', 'Step 3: Find Number of Sets: 2048 lines / 4 lines per set = 512 sets = 2^9 sets ⇒ Set bits = 9 bits.', 'Step 4: Calculate Tag bits: Total Address (32 bits) - Set bits (9) - Offset bits (5) = 32 - 14 = 18 bits.'], 'answer': 'Tag field size = 18 bits.'}], 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'GATE Trap: Write-Through vs Write-Back Cache', 'warning': "Write-Through updates both cache and RAM immediately (needs a Write Buffer). Write-Back updates only the cache block and sets a 'Dirty Bit'; RAM is updated only when that block is evicted!"}]}, ('COA', 3): {'sub_id': 'COA', 'chap_idx': 3, 'title': 'Pipeline Hazards, Speedup & Instruction Level Parallelism', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Example 1: The Laundromat Pipeline (Instruction Pipelining)', 'text': 'Doing 4 loads of laundry: Washer (30 mins), Dryer (40 mins), Folding (20 mins).\n• Non-pipelined: Wait until load 1 is completely washed, dried, and folded before starting load 2. Total time = 4 × 90 mins = 360 mins.\n• Pipelined: As soon as load 1 moves to the dryer, put load 2 into the washer! Each stage stays busy continuously. Total time shrinks dramatically to ~180 mins.'}, {'title': 'Real-Life Example 2: Waiting for the Oven (Data Hazard: RAW)', 'text': "You want to frost a cake (Instruction 2), but the cake is still baking in the oven (Instruction 1 hasn't written its result yet!).\nYou must wait (stall / bubble) until the cake cools down before you can frost it. In CPU design, Operand Forwarding is like taking the warm cake directly from the oven shelf instead of waiting for it to be packaged."}], 'foundations': [{'number': '12.1', 'title': 'The Three Classic Pipeline Hazards', 'content': "<b>1. Structural Hazard (Hardware Conflict):</b><br/>• Two instructions need the exact same physical resource at the same time (e.g. instruction fetch and data read both trying to access a single-port RAM).<br/>• Solution: Separate Instruction Cache and Data Cache (Harvard Architecture).<br/><br/><b>2. Data Hazard (RAW, WAR, WAW):</b><br/>• <b>RAW (Read After Write - True Dependency):</b> Inst 2 wants to read R1 before Inst 1 finishes writing to R1. Solved by <b>Operand Forwarding</b> or inserting NOP stalls.<br/>• <b>WAR (Write After Read - Anti-dependency):</b> Inst 2 writes before Inst 1 reads.<br/>• <b>WAW (Write After Write - Output dependency):</b> Inst 2 writes before Inst 1 writes, leaving old data.<br/><br/><b>3. Control Hazard (Branch Hazard):</b><br/>• Occurs on Conditional Jumps (<code>if/else</code>, <code>goto</code>) because the CPU doesn't know which path to fetch next.<br/>• Solution: Branch Prediction (Dynamic/Static) and Delayed Branching.", 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In a standard 5-stage pipeline without operand forwarding, a RAW dependency between consecutive instructions causes 2 stall cycles. With operand forwarding, stalls reduce to 0 (or 1 stall if dependent on a LOAD instruction)!'}, {'number': '12.2', 'title': 'Pipeline Clock Period, Speedup & CPI', 'content': '<b>Key Formulas to memorize:</b><br/>• <b>Clock Cycle Time (T_clk):</b> Max(Stage Delays) + Buffer Delay (Register Delay).<br/>• <b>Execution time for n instructions in k-stage pipeline:</b> Time = (k + n - 1) × T_clk.<br/>• <b>Ideal Speedup (when n is very large and no stalls):</b> Speedup = k (number of stages).<br/>• <b>Actual Speedup with stalls:</b> Speedup = (Non-pipelined time) / (Pipelined time) = (k × CPI_ideal) / (CPI_actual).', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: The slowest pipeline stage determines the clock frequency of the entire processor! Clock frequency = 1 / [Max(stage delay) + buffer delay].'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Calculating Pipeline Speedup', 'problem': 'A 5-stage pipeline has stage delays of 150 ps, 120 ps, 160 ps, 140 ps, and 110 ps. The pipeline register delay is 10 ps. What is the clock cycle time, and what is the speedup for 1000 instructions compared to non-pipelined execution?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Find the bottleneck stage: Max(150, 120, 160, 140, 110) = 160 ps.', 'Step 2: Add register delay: Clock Cycle (T_clk) = 160 ps + 10 ps = 170 ps.', 'Step 3: Non-pipelined time per instruction = Sum of stage delays = 150 + 120 + 160 + 140 + 110 = 680 ps.', 'Step 4: Non-pipelined total time for 1000 instructions = 1000 × 680 ps = 680,000 ps.', 'Step 5: Pipelined time for 1000 instructions = (k + n - 1) × T_clk = (5 + 1000 - 1) × 170 ps = 1004 × 170 = 170,680 ps.', 'Step 6: Speedup = 680,000 / 170,680 = 3.984 (close to theoretical 4x).'], 'answer': 'Clock Cycle = 170 ps, Speedup = 3.98x.'}], 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'GATE Trap: Average CPI with Stalls', 'warning': 'Average CPI = 1 + (Stalls per instruction). If 20% of instructions are branch instructions and each causes 2 stalls, CPI = 1 + (0.20 × 2) = 1.4!'}]}, ('PDS', 0): {'sub_id': 'PDS', 'chap_idx': 0, 'title': 'Programming in C & Recursion Tracing', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Example 1: Russian Nesting Dolls (Recursion & Call Stack)', 'text': 'Imagine opening Russian nesting dolls:\nYou open Doll #3 to find Doll #2 inside. You open Doll #2 to find Doll #1. At Doll #1, there are no more dolls inside: this is the Base Case!\nYou now stop opening and start assembling them back in reverse order. In computer memory, every recursive call pushes a frame onto the Call Stack. When the base case hits, frames pop off in Last-In First-Out (LIFO) order.'}, {'title': 'Real-Life Example 2: House Address on a Sticky Note (Pointers in C)', 'text': '• Pass by Value: You build an entire duplicate copy of your house and ship it to your friend. Wasteful and slow!\n• Pass by Reference (Pointers): You write your house address on a tiny sticky note (`int *p = &x`). Your friend reads the address, walks into your house, and paints the living room green (`*p = 50`). The original house is modified directly!'}, {'title': 'Real-Life Example 3: The Bolted Metal Locker (Static Variables in C)', 'text': 'Normally, when a function ends, all its local variables are erased from memory.\nA `static` variable is like a bolted metal locker: its value survives between function calls! The next time you call that function, it picks up with whatever number was left inside the locker.'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '13.1', 'title': 'Pointers & Array Equivalence in C (Explained Step by Step)', 'content': "In C language, pointers and arrays are closely connected. Here is how they work in simple point form:<br/><br/>• <b>1. What is an array name?</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- When you declare <code>int arr[5] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};</code>, the word <code>arr</code> is NOT the whole array.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- It is simply a pointer to its first element: <code>arr == &arr[0]</code>.<br/><br/>• <b>2. How does index notation arr[i] actually work?</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- In C, there is no separate index operator! C secretly translates <code>arr[i]</code> into pointer arithmetic:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>arr[i]</code> &nbsp;≡&nbsp; <code>*(arr + i)</code><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Since addition is commutative (<code>a + b == b + a</code>), <code>*(arr + i)</code> is identical to <code>*(i + arr)</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Surprising C Fact:</b> You can legally write <code>3[arr]</code> in C, and it gives the exact same value as <code>arr[3]</code>!<br/><br/>• <b>3. The Big Difference: *p++ vs (*p)++</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <code>*p++</code>: Moves the pointer! The <code>++</code> is attached to <code>p</code>. It reads the current value, then advances pointer <code>p</code> to point to the next integer in memory.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <code>(*p)++</code>: Modifies the value! The parentheses protect <code>p</code>. The pointer stays at the same memory box, but increments the integer value inside that box by 1 (e.g. 10 becomes 11).<br/><br/>• <b>4. What is Array Decay?</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- When you pass an array into a function (like <code>solve(arr)</code>), C never copies the whole array.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- The array 'decays' into a simple pointer to the first element.", 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Because arrays decay to pointers inside functions, running sizeof(arr) inside a function returns the size of a pointer (4 bytes on 32-bit, 8 bytes on 64-bit), NOT the total bytes of the array! Always pass array length as a separate argument.'}, {'number': '13.2', 'title': 'Short-Circuit Evaluation in Logical Expressions', 'content': 'C evaluates logical expressions from left to right and stops as soon as the outcome is certain:<br/><br/>• <b>In Logical AND (A && B):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If <code>A</code> evaluates to False (0), the entire condition is guaranteed to be False.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Therefore, <b>B is NEVER EXECUTED</b>!<br/><br/>• <b>In Logical OR (A || B):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If <code>A</code> evaluates to True (non-zero), the entire condition is guaranteed to be True.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Therefore, <b>B is NEVER EXECUTED</b>!', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Watch out for increment operators inside logical checks! Example: in if (a == 0 && ++b > 0), if a is 1 (False), the second expression ++b is skipped entirely, leaving b unchanged!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Tracing Static Recursion', 'problem': 'What is printed by this C program?\nint f(int n) {\n  static int r = 0;\n  if (n <= 0) return 1;\n  if (n > 3) { r = n; return f(n - 2) + 2; }\n  return f(n - 1) + r;\n}\nint main() { printf("%d", f(5)); }', 'steps': ['Step 1: Call f(5). Here n = 5 (> 3). Static r is updated to 5. It returns f(3) + 2.', 'Step 2: Now call f(3). Here n = 3 (not > 3). It goes to line: return f(2) + r.', 'Step 3: Call f(2). It returns f(1) + r.', 'Step 4: Call f(1). It returns f(0) + r.', 'Step 5: Call f(0). Here n <= 0, so it hits the base case and returns 1.', 'Step 6: Now unwind the call stack (remember static r is STILL 5!):', '  - f(1) = f(0) + r = 1 + 5 = 6.', '  - f(2) = f(1) + r = 6 + 5 = 11.', '  - f(3) = f(2) + r = 11 + 5 = 16.', 'Step 7: Finally compute original return: f(5) = f(3) + 2 = 16 + 2 = 18.'], 'answer': 'Final printed output = 18.'}], 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'GATE Trap: Multiple Modifications in One Expression', 'warning': 'Writing expressions like `i = i++ + ++i` modifies variable `i` multiple times between sequence points. According to the ISO C standard, this is UNDEFINED BEHAVIOR, and questions asking for a fixed output are technically invalid or ambiguous!'}]}, ('PDS', 1): {'sub_id': 'PDS', 'chap_idx': 1, 'title': 'Linear Data Structures - Stacks, Queues & Linked Lists', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Example 1: Browser Back Button vs Ticket Counter (Stack vs Queue)', 'text': "• Stack (LIFO: Last-In First-Out): Like a stack of dinner plates. The last plate placed on top is the first one picked up. The browser 'Back' button uses a stack to pop the most recent website you visited.\n• Queue (FIFO: First-In First-Out): Like a movie ticket counter. The first person in line gets tickets first. Printer queues work the same way so the first print job sent is printed first."}, {'title': 'Real-Life Example 2: Circular Dining Table (Circular Queue)', 'text': "In a regular linear queue, when people leave from the front, empty seats open up, but you can't seat new people if the rear reached the end of the room.\nA Circular Queue connects the last seat back to the first seat using modulo arithmetic `(rear + 1) % Capacity`, ensuring every single empty seat can be reused."}, {'title': 'Real-Life Example 3: Treasure Hunt Clue Slips (Linked Lists)', 'text': 'An array is like 10 adjacent hotel lockers: super fast lookup by number, but impossible to expand if neighboring lockers are occupied.\nA Linked List is like a treasure hunt: each clue card holds an item and the GPS coordinate of the next clue. You can insert a new clue anywhere in O(1) time without moving other clues!'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '14.1', 'title': 'Infix, Postfix & Stack Conversions', 'content': '<b>Why do computers prefer Postfix notation?</b><br/>• Human notation (Infix): <code>A + B * C</code> requires remembering operator precedence rules and parentheses.<br/>• Computer notation (Postfix / Reverse Polish): <code>A B C * +</code> requires zero parentheses and can be evaluated in a single pass using a stack!<br/><br/><b>How to evaluate Postfix using a Stack:</b><br/>1. Scan symbols from left to right.<br/>2. If you see an operand (number), push it onto the stack.<br/>3. If you see an operator (+, -, *, /):<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Pop the top element as <b>Operand 2</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Pop the next element as <b>Operand 1</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Calculate: <code>Result = Operand 1 (operator) Operand 2</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Push the Result back onto the stack.<br/>4. When the expression ends, the stack top holds the final answer.', 'crucial_rule': "GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: When popping two operands for non-commutative operators like '-' and '/': The FIRST popped item is Operand 2, and the SECOND popped item is Operand 1! Result = Op1 / Op2 (NOT Op2 / Op1)."}, {'number': '14.2', 'title': 'Circular Queue Full and Empty Conditions', 'content': 'In an array of size N implementing a circular queue:<br/>• <b>Empty Condition:</b> <code>front == rear</code>.<br/>• <b>Full Condition (sacrificing 1 slot to distinguish from empty):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>(rear + 1) % N == front</code>.<br/>• Maximum elements the queue can hold = <b>N - 1</b>.', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: If an array has size N = 8, a circular queue can store at most 7 elements without confusing the Full condition with the Empty condition!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Evaluating Postfix Expression', 'problem': 'Evaluate the postfix expression: 6  2  3  +  *  10  -', 'steps': ["Step 1: Read '6' (number) ⇒ Push to stack: [6].", "Step 2: Read '2' (number) ⇒ Push to stack: [6, 2].", "Step 3: Read '3' (number) ⇒ Push to stack: [6, 2, 3].", "Step 4: Read '+' (operator) ⇒ Pop 3 (Op2), Pop 2 (Op1). Calculate 2 + 3 = 5. Push 5: [6, 5].", "Step 5: Read '*' (operator) ⇒ Pop 5 (Op2), Pop 6 (Op1). Calculate 6 * 5 = 30. Push 30: [30].", "Step 6: Read '10' (number) ⇒ Push to stack: [30, 10].", "Step 7: Read '-' (operator) ⇒ Pop 10 (Op2), Pop 30 (Op1). Calculate 30 - 10 = 20. Push 20: [20]."], 'answer': 'Final evaluated result = 20.'}], 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'GATE Trap: Reversing a Singly Linked List', 'warning': 'To reverse a singly linked list in-place in O(n) time and O(1) space, you need exactly 3 pointers: `prev = NULL`, `curr = head`, and `next = NULL`. Forgetting to save `curr->next` before redirecting the link breaks the list!'}]}, ('PDS', 2): {'sub_id': 'PDS', 'chap_idx': 2, 'title': 'Trees, Binary Search Trees & Binary Heaps', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Example 1: The Hospital Emergency Triage (Priority Queue & Binary Heap)', 'text': 'In a hospital emergency room, patients are not treated in first-come first-served order. A patient with a heart attack is treated immediately ahead of someone with a sprained ankle!\nA Binary Heap (Max-Heap) guarantees that the most critical element is always at the root (index 0), allowing the doctors to extract the highest priority patient in O(log n) time.'}, {'title': 'Real-Life Example 2: Searching a Dictionary (Binary Search Tree - BST)', 'text': 'In a BST, every node follows one golden rule: All values in the left branch are smaller, and all values in the right branch are larger.\nTo look up any word, you compare and throw away half of the tree at every step, just like opening a physical dictionary right in the middle.'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '15.1', 'title': 'Binary Tree Fundamental Properties', 'content': 'Here are the core mathematical properties every GATE aspirant must know:<br/><br/>• <b>Handshaking Lemma for Trees:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;In any binary tree, the number of leaf nodes (L) is always 1 more than the number of nodes with two children (n2):<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>L = n2 + 1</b>.<br/><br/>• <b>Tree Traversals:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Pre-order: Root → Left → Right.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- In-order: Left → Root → Right (<b>Always yields strictly sorted order in a BST!</b>).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Post-order: Left → Right → Root.<br/><br/>• <b>Reconstructing a Unique Tree:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;To reconstruct a unique binary tree, you MUST be given <b>In-order</b> along with either Pre-order or Post-order.', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In-order traversal of a Binary Search Tree (BST) is ALWAYS strictly sorted in ascending order! If an exam question gives only Pre-order traversal of a BST, you can find the In-order yourself simply by sorting the numbers!'}, {'number': '15.2', 'title': 'Binary Heap Array Indexing & Build-Heap Complexity', 'content': 'A binary heap is a Complete Binary Tree stored inside a simple array without pointers:<br/><br/>• For a node at index <code>i</code> (0-based indexing):<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Left Child = <code>2i + 1</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Right Child = <code>2i + 2</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Parent = <code>floor((i - 1) / 2)</code>.<br/><br/>• <b>Build-Heap Algorithm Time Complexity:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Building a heap of size n from an unsorted array takes <b>O(n) time</b> (linear time), NOT O(n log n)!', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Inserting 1 element into a heap takes O(log n). But building a full heap of n elements from scratch takes O(n) using the bottom-up Build-Heap procedure!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Tree Reconstruction', 'problem': 'Given Preorder: [A, B, D, E, C, F] and Inorder: [D, B, E, A, F, C]. Find the Postorder traversal.', 'steps': ["Step 1: First element of Preorder is root ⇒ Root = 'A'.", "Step 2: Find 'A' in Inorder: [D, B, E] are in Left subtree, [F, C] are in Right subtree.", "Step 3: In Left subtree [D, B, E], Preorder has 'B' first ⇒ Root of left subtree = 'B'. Inorder [D, B, E] means 'D' is left of B, 'E' is right of B.", "Step 4: In Right subtree [F, C], Preorder has 'C' first ⇒ Root of right subtree = 'C'. Inorder [F, C] means 'F' is left child of C.", 'Step 5: Write Postorder (Left, Right, Root):', '  - Left branch of A: D, E, B.', '  - Right branch of A: F, C.', '  - Root: A.'], 'answer': 'Postorder = [D, E, B, F, C, A].'}], 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'GATE Trap: Number of Unique BSTs with n keys', 'warning': 'The number of structurally unique Binary Search Trees that can be formed with n distinct keys is given by the n-th Catalan Number: C_n = (1 / (n + 1)) × C(2n, n). For n = 3: C_3 = 5. For n = 4: C_4 = 14.'}]}, ('ALGO', 0): {'sub_id': 'ALGO', 'chap_idx': 0, 'title': 'Asymptotic Analysis, Master Theorem & Recurrences', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Example 1: Carrier Pigeon vs Internet Bandwidth (Asymptotic Growth)', 'text': 'If you send a 16 GB flash drive strapped to a carrier pigeon, the pigeon takes 1 hour to fly across the city regardless of whether the drive has 1 GB or 1000 GB (O(1) constant time!).\nIf you send it over a 10 Mbps internet connection, time grows linearly with data size (O(n)). For small files, the internet wins. But as data grows to petabytes, the pigeon asymptotically beats the internet! Big-O measures this behavior as n grows towards infinity.'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '16.1', 'title': 'Big-O, Big-Omega & Big-Theta Explained', 'content': "Asymptotic notations describe how running time scales as input size n grows huge:<br/><br/>• <b>Big-O (O): Upper Bound (Worst-case ceiling)</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <code>f(n) = O(g(n))</code> means f(n) will never grow faster than c × g(n).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Plain English: 'The algorithm will take at most this much time.'<br/><br/>• <b>Big-Omega (Ω): Lower Bound (Best-case floor)</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <code>f(n) = Ω(g(n))</code> means f(n) will take at least c × g(n).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Plain English: 'The algorithm will take at least this much time.'<br/><br/>• <b>Big-Theta (Θ): Tight Bound (Exact growth rate)</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <code>f(n) = Θ(g(n))</code> holds if and only if f(n) is BOTH O(g(n)) and Ω(g(n)).", 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Common growth order from slowest to fastest: O(1) < O(log log n) < O(log n) < O(sqrt(n)) < O(n) < O(n log n) < O(n^2) < O(2^n) < O(n!). Remember that (log n)^1000 grows slower than n^0.001 as n reaches infinity!'}, {'number': '16.2', 'title': 'Master Theorem for Divide and Conquer', 'content': 'For recurrence relations of the form: <b>T(n) = a T(n/b) + Θ(n^k log^p n)</b>:<br/>Compare <code>a</code> with <code>b^k</code>:<br/><br/>• <b>Case 1: If a > b^k</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Roots dominate! T(n) = <b>Θ(n^(log_b a))</b>.<br/><br/>• <b>Case 2: If a == b^k</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;T(n) = <b>Θ(n^k log^(p+1) n)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;(For standard mergesort where p = 0: T(n) = Θ(n log n)).<br/><br/>• <b>Case 3: If a < b^k</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Leaves dominate! T(n) = <b>Θ(n^k log^p n)</b>.', 'crucial_rule': "GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Master theorem cannot be applied if: (1) 'a' is not a constant (e.g. T(n) = 2^n T(n/2)), (2) 'b' is a subtraction (e.g. T(n) = T(n-1) + 1), or (3) f(n) is not polynomial."}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Solving Recurrence with Master Theorem', 'problem': 'Find the asymptotic time complexity of T(n) = 8 T(n/2) + n^2.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Identify parameters: a = 8, b = 2, f(n) = n^2 (so k = 2, p = 0).', 'Step 2: Calculate b^k = 2^2 = 4.', "Step 3: Compare 'a' and 'b^k': a = 8, b^k = 4. Since 8 > 4, Case 1 applies!", 'Step 4: Compute log_b(a) = log_2(8) = 3.', 'Step 5: Apply Case 1 formula: T(n) = Θ(n^(log_b a)) = Θ(n^3).'], 'answer': 'Time Complexity = Θ(n^3).'}], 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'GATE Trap: Decreasing Recurrence Relations', 'warning': 'For recurrences like T(n) = T(n-1) + n, do NOT use Master Theorem! Use iterative expansion: T(n) = n + (n-1) + (n-2) + ... + 1 = n(n+1)/2 = Θ(n^2).'}]}, ('ALGO', 1): {'sub_id': 'ALGO', 'chap_idx': 1, 'title': 'Sorting Algorithms, Lower Bounds & Hash Tables', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Example 1: Arranging Playing Cards (Insertion Sort vs Quick Sort)', 'text': 'When you pick up playing cards one by one into your hand, you slide each new card into its sorted place among the cards you already hold: this is Insertion Sort (O(n) best-case for nearly sorted cards!).\nWhen sorting a huge pile of 10,000 exam sheets, you pick a pivot roll number (like 5000), split into piles < 5000 and > 5000, and repeat. This is Quick Sort.'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '17.1', 'title': 'Master Summary of Sorting Algorithms', 'content': 'Here is the definitive comparison table tested in GATE:<br/><br/>• <b>Merge Sort:</b> Best: O(n log n) | Worst: O(n log n) | Space: O(n) | Stable: YES.<br/>• <b>Heap Sort:</b> Best: O(n log n) | Worst: O(n log n) | Space: O(1) in-place | Stable: NO.<br/>• <b>Quick Sort:</b> Best: O(n log n) | Worst: O(n^2) (sorted input) | Space: O(log n) | Stable: NO.<br/>• <b>Insertion Sort:</b> Best: O(n) (already sorted) | Worst: O(n^2) | Space: O(1) | Stable: YES.<br/>• <b>Counting Sort:</b> Time: O(n + k) | Space: O(n + k) | Non-comparison based | Stable: YES.', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Any comparison-based sorting algorithm requires at least Ω(n log n) comparisons in the worst case! To sort faster than O(n log n), you must use non-comparison algorithms like Counting Sort or Radix Sort.'}, {'number': '17.2', 'title': 'Hash Table Collisions: Chaining vs Open Addressing', 'content': '<b>What is a Collision?</b><br/>Two distinct keys produce the same hash slot: <code>h(k1) == h(k2)</code>.<br/><br/><b>1. Separate Chaining:</b><br/>• Each slot points to a linked list. Can store infinite elements. Load factor α can exceed 1.<br/><br/><b>2. Open Addressing (All keys stored inside the table itself; α <= 1):</b><br/>• Linear Probing: <code>h(k, i) = (h(k) + i) % m</code>. Problem: Primary Clustering (long continuous runs of occupied slots).<br/>• Quadratic Probing: <code>h(k, i) = (h(k) + c1*i + c2*i^2) % m</code>. Solves primary clustering; suffers from secondary clustering.<br/>• Double Hashing: <code>h(k, i) = (h1(k) + i * h2(k)) % m</code>. Best open addressing method; eliminates clustering.', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In Double Hashing, h2(k) must be relatively prime to the table size m (e.g. choose m as a prime number) so the probe sequence visits every single slot in the table!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Linear Probing Hash Table', 'problem': 'Insert keys [12, 22, 32, 42] into a hash table of size 10 using h(k) = k % 10 with Linear Probing. Find the slot where 42 is stored.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Key 12: 12 % 10 = 2. Slot 2 is empty ⇒ Insert 12 at Slot 2.', 'Step 2: Key 22: 22 % 10 = 2. Slot 2 is occupied! Probe (2 + 1) % 10 = 3. Slot 3 is empty ⇒ Insert 22 at Slot 3.', 'Step 3: Key 32: 32 % 10 = 2. Slot 2 is occupied, Slot 3 is occupied! Probe (2 + 2) % 10 = 4. Slot 4 is empty ⇒ Insert 32 at Slot 4.', 'Step 4: Key 42: 42 % 10 = 2. Slots 2, 3, 4 are occupied! Probe (2 + 3) % 10 = 5. Slot 5 is empty ⇒ Insert 42 at Slot 5.'], 'answer': 'Key 42 is stored at Slot index 5.'}], 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'GATE Trap: Deletion in Open Addressing', 'warning': "In open addressing, you CANNOT simply erase an element and leave the slot empty! Leaving it empty will cause subsequent searches to terminate prematurely. You must mark deleted slots with a special 'DELETED' tombstone flag."}]}, ('ALGO', 2): {'sub_id': 'ALGO', 'chap_idx': 2, 'title': 'Greedy Algorithms, Dynamic Programming & Divide-and-Conquer', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Example 1: Indian Rupee Coin Change (When Greedy Fails)', 'text': 'With Indian currency denominations [₹1, ₹2, ₹5, ₹10], making change for ₹18 greedily (pick largest first: 10 + 5 + 2 + 1 = 4 coins) always gives the optimal answer.\nHowever, with custom coins like [₹1, ₹7, ₹10], to make ₹14:\n• Greedy picks: ₹10 + ₹1 + ₹1 + ₹1 + ₹1 = 5 coins.\n• Optimal Dynamic Programming picks: ₹7 + ₹7 = 2 coins!\nThis proves Greedy choice fails unless the coin system has a specific mathematical property.'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '18.1', 'title': 'Greedy Method vs Dynamic Programming', 'content': '• <b>Greedy Method:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Makes the locally best choice at every step without ever backtracking.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Fast (typically O(n log n)), but only works if problem satisfies: (1) Greedy Choice Property, and (2) Optimal Substructure.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Examples: Fractional Knapsack, Kruskal MST, Prim MST, Dijkstra, Huffman Coding.<br/><br/>• <b>Dynamic Programming (DP):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Explores all subproblems but avoids redundant calculations by saving results in a table.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Required properties: (1) Optimal Substructure, and (2) <b>Overlapping Subproblems</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Examples: 0/1 Knapsack, Longest Common Subsequence (LCS), Matrix Chain Multiplication, Floyd-Warshall.', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Fractional Knapsack is solved in O(n log n) by Greedy (sort by value/weight ratio). But 0/1 Knapsack CANNOT be solved greedily; it requires Dynamic Programming in O(n * W) pseudo-polynomial time!'}, {'number': '18.2', 'title': 'Longest Common Subsequence (LCS) Recurrence', 'content': 'Given two strings X of length m and Y of length n:<br/>• If last characters match (<code>X[m-1] == Y[n-1]</code>):<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>LCS(X, Y) = 1 + LCS(X[0..m-2], Y[0..n-2])</code>.<br/>• If characters do NOT match:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>LCS(X, Y) = Max(LCS(X[0..m-2], Y), LCS(X, Y[0..n-2]))</code>.<br/>• Time Complexity: <b>O(m × n)</b> using a 2D table.', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Matrix Chain Multiplication: To multiply matrices A1(p0×p1), A2(p1×p2)... An(pn-1×pn), the minimum scalar multiplications can be found in O(n^3) time using dynamic programming.'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Fractional Knapsack', 'problem': 'Knapsack capacity W = 50 kg. Items: A (v=60, w=10), B (v=100, w=20), C (v=120, w=30). Find maximum value.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Compute value-to-weight ratio for each item:', '  - Item A: 60 / 10 = 6.0 per kg.', '  - Item B: 100 / 20 = 5.0 per kg.', '  - Item C: 120 / 30 = 4.0 per kg.', 'Step 2: Pick highest ratio first (Item A): Take full 10 kg. Value = 60. Remaining capacity = 50 - 10 = 40 kg.', 'Step 3: Next pick Item B: Take full 20 kg. Value = 60 + 100 = 160. Remaining capacity = 40 - 20 = 20 kg.', 'Step 4: Next pick Item C: Only 20 kg capacity left! Take fraction (20 / 30) of Item C. Value = 160 + (2/3 × 120) = 160 + 80 = 240.'], 'answer': 'Maximum attainable value = 240.'}], 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'GATE Trap: Huffman Code Tree Uniqueness', 'warning': 'Huffman code lengths and total bits for the message are ALWAYS optimal and unique. However, the exact binary codes (0 vs 1 assignment to left/right branches) are NOT unique!'}]}, ('ALGO', 3): {'sub_id': 'ALGO', 'chap_idx': 3, 'title': 'Graph Traversals, MST & Shortest Paths', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': "Real-Life Example 1: Google Maps Navigation (Dijkstra's Algorithm)", 'text': "When Google Maps finds the fastest route from your home to college, cities and intersections are vertices, and roads with traffic delays are weighted edges.\nDijkstra's algorithm expands outward like a ripple in a pond, always visiting the closest unvisited intersection first, finding the absolute shortest path in O((V + E) log V) time."}, {'title': 'Real-Life Example 2: Connecting Cities with Electric Power Lines (Minimum Spanning Tree)', 'text': "A power company wants to electrify 10 cities with the minimum total kilometers of wire.\nYou don't need a direct cable between every single city (no cycles needed!). You just need a Minimum Spanning Tree (MST): exactly V - 1 cables connecting all V cities at the lowest possible cost."}], 'foundations': [{'number': '19.1', 'title': 'BFS vs DFS Comparison', 'content': '• <b>Breadth-First Search (BFS):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Data Structure: <b>Queue (FIFO)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Explores all immediate neighbors first, layer by layer.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Key Application: Finds the <b>shortest path in an unweighted graph</b>!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Time Complexity: <code>O(V + E)</code> using adjacency list.<br/><br/>• <b>Depth-First Search (DFS):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Data Structure: <b>Stack (LIFO / Recursion)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Explores deep down a branch until it hits a dead end, then backtracks.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Key Applications: Cycle detection, Topological Sorting, finding Strongly Connected Components (Tarjan/Kosaraju).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Time Complexity: <code>O(V + E)</code>.', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In DFS of an undirected graph, every edge is either a Tree Edge or a Back Edge (Cross edges NEVER exist in undirected DFS)! A Back Edge indicates the presence of a cycle.'}, {'number': '19.2', 'title': 'Dijkstra vs Bellman-Ford vs Floyd-Warshall', 'content': "• <b>Dijkstra's Algorithm (Single Source Shortest Path):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Condition: Edge weights must be <b>NON-NEGATIVE</b> (fails on negative weights!).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Time: <code>O((V + E) log V)</code> with Min-Heap.<br/><br/>• <b>Bellman-Ford Algorithm (Single Source Shortest Path):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Handles negative weights and detects negative weight cycles.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Time: <code>O(V × E)</code>.<br/><br/>• <b>Floyd-Warshall Algorithm (All-Pairs Shortest Path):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Dynamic programming approach computing shortest paths between all pairs of nodes.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Time: <code>O(V^3)</code>.", 'crucial_rule': "GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Prim's algorithm and Dijkstra's algorithm look almost identical, but their relaxation step differs: Dijkstra updates d[v] = min(d[v], d[u] + weight(u,v)). Prim updates key[v] = min(key[v], weight(u,v))!"}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': "Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Kruskal's MST Algorithm", 'problem': 'A graph has vertices {A, B, C, D} and edges: (A-B: 1), (B-C: 2), (A-C: 3), (C-D: 4), (B-D: 5). Find the total weight of the MST.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Sort all edges in non-decreasing order of weight:\n  (A-B: 1), (B-C: 2), (A-C: 3), (C-D: 4), (B-D: 5).', 'Step 2: Pick smallest edge (A-B, wt 1). Add to MST. (Edges = 1).', 'Step 3: Pick next edge (B-C, wt 2). Add to MST. (Edges = 2).', 'Step 4: Pick next edge (A-C, wt 3). Both A and C are already in the same connected set! Adding A-C creates a cycle (A-B-C-A). Discard A-C!', 'Step 5: Pick next edge (C-D, wt 4). D is not yet connected. Add to MST. (Edges = 3).', 'Step 6: We have selected V - 1 = 4 - 1 = 3 edges. MST is complete!\n  Total MST weight = 1 + 2 + 4 = 7.'], 'answer': 'Minimum Spanning Tree Total Weight = 7.'}], 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'GATE Trap: Topological Sort', 'warning': 'Topological Sort is ONLY possible on a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG). If a graph has even one directed cycle, a valid topological sort does NOT exist!'}]}, ('TOC', 0): {'sub_id': 'TOC', 'chap_idx': 0, 'title': 'Finite Automata, Regular Expressions & Minimization', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Example 1: The Soda Vending Machine (Finite Automaton)', 'text': 'A can of soda costs ₹20. The machine accepts ₹5 and ₹10 coins.\nThe machine does not have an infinite hard drive. It only needs to remember 4 simple states: {₹0, ₹5, ₹10, ₹15}.\nWhen you insert coins and reach state ₹20, the can drops (Final / Accepting State!).\nBecause the machine needs only a small, fixed number of states, it is a Deterministic Finite Automaton (DFA).'}, {'title': 'Real-Life Example 2: Email Format Validator (Regular Expressions)', 'text': "When you register on an app, a Regular Expression checks whether your text has letters, an '@' symbol, and a domain like '.com'.\nYour web browser compiles that regex into a tiny DFA and tests each character in microseconds. If it ends in an accepting state, a green checkmark appears."}], 'foundations': [{'number': '20.1', 'title': 'DFA vs NFA Equivalences', 'content': '• <b>DFA (Deterministic Finite Automaton):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- For every state and input symbol, there is <b>EXACTLY ONE</b> next state.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- No ε-moves (cannot change state without reading input).<br/><br/>• <b>NFA (Non-Deterministic Finite Automaton):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Can have 0, 1, or multiple transitions on the same input symbol, plus optional ε-transitions.<br/><br/>• <b>Equivalent Power:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>DFA and NFA have the EXACT SAME expressive power!</b> Both recognize the exact set of Regular Languages.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Any NFA with n states can be converted into an equivalent DFA with at most <b>2^n states</b>.', 'crucial_rule': "GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: For the language 'all binary strings where the n-th symbol from the right is 1': The NFA needs only (n + 1) states, but the minimal DFA strictly requires 2^n states!"}, {'number': '20.2', 'title': 'Regular Language Closure Properties', 'content': 'Regular languages are closed under <b>ALMOST EVERY standard operation</b>:<br/><br/>• Union (L1 ∪ L2): <b>Closed ✔</b><br/>• Intersection (L1 ∩ L2): <b>Closed ✔</b><br/>• Complement (~L): <b>Closed ✔</b><br/>• Concatenation (L1 · L2): <b>Closed ✔</b><br/>• Kleene Star (L*): <b>Closed ✔</b><br/>• Reversal (L^R): <b>Closed ✔</b><br/>• Homomorphism & Substitution: <b>Closed ✔</b>', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: To find the complement of a DFA: (1) Make sure the DFA is complete with all dead/trap states included, then (2) Swap Final states and Non-final states!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': "Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Arden's Theorem", 'problem': 'Solve the regular expression equation: R = b + R a.', 'steps': ["Step 1: State Arden's Theorem: If P and Q are regular expressions, and P does not contain ε, then the equation R = Q + R P has a UNIQUE solution: R = Q P*.", 'Step 2: Compare R = b + R a with R = Q + R P.', "Step 3: Identify terms: Q = b, and P = a (since P = a does not contain ε, Arden's rule applies).", 'Step 4: Write unique solution: R = Q P* = b a*.'], 'answer': 'Solution: R = b a*.'}], 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'GATE Trap: Pumping Lemma', 'warning': 'Pumping Lemma is used ONLY to prove a language is NOT regular. You can NEVER use Pumping Lemma to prove a language IS regular!'}]}, ('TOC', 1): {'sub_id': 'TOC', 'chap_idx': 1, 'title': 'Context-Free Grammars, Pushdown Automata & Chomsky Hierarchy', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Example 1: Matching Parentheses in VS Code (Pushdown Automaton)', 'text': 'When you write code with nested brackets `(( { [ ] } ))`, how does your code editor know that every opening bracket has a matching closing bracket?\nA finite automaton cannot do this because it cannot count to infinity! It needs an auxiliary Stack memory.\nPush each opening bracket onto the stack; when a closing bracket arrives, pop and verify. This is a Pushdown Automaton (PDA).'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '21.1', 'title': 'Deterministic PDA vs Non-Deterministic PDA', 'content': "• <b>DPDA (Deterministic Pushdown Automata):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Recognizes <b>Deterministic Context-Free Languages (DCFL)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- DCFLs are unambiguous and parsed efficiently in linear time by compilers.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Example: <code>{ a^n b^n | n >= 1 }</code>, balanced parentheses, programming languages.<br/><br/>• <b>NPDA (Non-Deterministic Pushdown Automata):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Recognizes <b>ALL Context-Free Languages (CFL)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>NPDA is strictly more powerful than DPDA!</b> (Unlike finite automata where NFA == DFA).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Example of CFL that is NOT DCFL: Even palindromes <code>{ w w^R }</code> (because you don't know where the middle is without guessing!).", 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: DPDA is NOT equivalent to NPDA! DCFL is a strict proper subset of CFL: Regular ⊂ DCFL ⊂ CFL ⊂ CSL ⊂ Recursive ⊂ RE.'}, {'number': '21.2', 'title': 'CFL Closure Properties (The Famous Exceptions!)', 'content': "Context-Free Languages are closed under Union, Concatenation, and Kleene Star.<br/><br/><b>The Two Famous Non-Closure Exceptions:</b><br/>• <b>Intersection: NOT CLOSED ✘</b> (The intersection of two CFLs is not necessarily context-free!).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Example: L1 = { a^n b^n c^m } (CFL) ∩ L2 = { a^m b^n c^n } (CFL) = { a^n b^n c^n } (CSL, NOT CFL!).<br/><br/>• <b>Complement: NOT CLOSED ✘</b> (If CFL were closed under complement, by De Morgan's laws it would be closed under intersection).", 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: While CFL is not closed under intersection with another CFL, the intersection of a CFL with a REGULAR language is ALWAYS a CFL!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Language Classification', 'problem': 'Classify the language L = { a^n b^m c^k | n = m or m = k }.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Notice the condition: n = m OR m = k.', 'Step 2: L can be split into L1 ∪ L2, where L1 = { a^n b^n c^k } (CFL) and L2 = { a^n b^m c^m } (CFL).', 'Step 3: Because CFLs are closed under Union, L = L1 ∪ L2 is guaranteed to be a Context-Free Language (CFL).', "Step 4: However, because it is an inherently ambiguous union, it cannot be recognized deterministically without guessing whether to match a's with b's or b's with c's."], 'answer': 'L is a Context-Free Language (CFL), but NOT a Deterministic CFL (DCFL).'}], 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'GATE Trap: DCFL Complement', 'warning': 'While general CFLs are NOT closed under complement, Deterministic CFLs (DCFL) ARE closed under complement!'}]}, ('TOC', 2): {'sub_id': 'TOC', 'chap_idx': 2, 'title': "Turing Machines, Decidability & Rice's Theorem", 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Example 1: The Infinite Loop Detector (The Halting Problem)', 'text': "Can you write a master antivirus or debugging tool that takes any C program as input and tells you with 100% certainty: 'Will this program ever finish, or will it loop forever?'\nAlan Turing proved that such a program is mathematically impossible! If it existed, you could feed it a paradoxical program that halts if and only if the checker says it loops, creating an unresolvable contradiction."}], 'foundations': [{'number': '22.1', 'title': 'Recursive vs Recursively Enumerable Languages', 'content': '• <b>Recursive Language (Decidable):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- A Turing Machine exists that halts on ALL inputs (halts and says YES if string is in language; halts and says NO if string is not).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Closed under complement</b>: If L is Recursive, ~L is also Recursive.<br/><br/>• <b>Recursively Enumerable Language - RE (Semi-Decidable):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If string is in L, Turing Machine halts and says YES.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If string is NOT in L, Turing Machine may say NO, or <b>loop forever</b>!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If both L and ~L are RE, then L is strictly <b>Recursive</b>!', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Halting Problem of Turing Machine (HP) is Recursively Enumerable (RE) but UNDECIDABLE. The complement of the Halting Problem is NOT even Recursively Enumerable!'}, {'number': '22.2', 'title': "Rice's Theorem for Turing Machines", 'content': "<b>Rice's Theorem states:</b><br/>Any non-trivial semantic property of the language recognized by a Turing Machine is <b>UNDECIDABLE</b>.<br/><br/>• <b>What is a Semantic Property?</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;A property about the language L(M) that the machine recognizes (e.g. 'Is L(M) empty?', 'Is L(M) regular?', 'Does L(M) contain palindrome?').<br/><br/>• <b>What does Non-trivial mean?</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Some Turing machines have this property, and some do not.", 'crucial_rule': "GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: If a question asks 'Is the language of Turing Machine M regular/finite/empty/CFL?', the answer is instantly UNDECIDABLE by Rice's Theorem!"}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Decidability Verification', 'problem': 'Which of the following is decidable? (A) Emptiness of DFA, (B) Emptiness of TM, (C) Equivalence of CFG, (D) Ambiguity of CFG.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Check (A) Emptiness of DFA: Solved by checking graph reachability from start state to any final state using BFS in O(V + E) time ⇒ DECIDABLE!', "Step 2: Check (B) Emptiness of TM: Undecidable by Rice's Theorem.", 'Step 3: Check (C) Equivalence of CFG: Undecidable (cannot check if L(G1) == L(G2)).', 'Step 4: Check (D) Ambiguity of CFG: Undecidable by reduction from PCP.'], 'answer': 'Only (A) Emptiness of DFA is Decidable.'}], 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'GATE Trap: Membership in CFG', 'warning': "Membership in CFG ('Does string w belong to CFG G?') IS DECIDABLE! It can be verified in O(n^3) time using the CYK algorithm."}]}, ('CD', 0): {'sub_id': 'CD', 'chap_idx': 0, 'title': 'Lexical Analysis, FIRST & FOLLOW Sets', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Example 1: Reading Sentences vs Grammar Checking (Lexer vs Parser)', 'text': 'When reading an English sentence:\n• Lexical Analyzer (Scanner): Groups individual letters into words (Tokens): [Noun, Verb, Adjective]. Whitespaces are discarded.\n• Syntax Analyzer (Parser): Checks whether the sequence of words follows grammatical rules (Sentence = Subject + Verb + Object).'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '23.1', 'title': 'Rules for Calculating FIRST Sets', 'content': 'For any grammar symbol X:<br/>• 1. If X is a terminal, <code>FIRST(X) = { X }</code>.<br/>• 2. If X → ε is a production, add <code>ε</code> to <code>FIRST(X)</code>.<br/>• 3. If X is non-terminal and X → Y1 Y2 Y3...:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Add all terminals from <code>FIRST(Y1)</code> (except ε) to <code>FIRST(X)</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If Y1 can produce ε, add <code>FIRST(Y2)</code> as well, continuing until a symbol does NOT produce ε.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If all Y1, Y2... produce ε, add <code>ε</code> to <code>FIRST(X)</code>.', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: ε can appear in a FIRST set, but ε NEVER appears in a FOLLOW set!'}, {'number': '23.2', 'title': 'Rules for Calculating FOLLOW Sets', 'content': 'For non-terminal A:<br/>• 1. If A is the Start Symbol, add <b>$ (end-marker)</b> to <code>FOLLOW(A)</code>.<br/>• 2. If there is a production <code>B → α A β</code>:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Everything in <code>FIRST(β)</code> (except ε) goes into <code>FOLLOW(A)</code>.<br/>• 3. If there is a production <code>B → α A</code>, or <code>B → α A β</code> where <code>FIRST(β)</code> contains ε:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Everything in <code>FOLLOW(B)</code> goes into <code>FOLLOW(A)</code>.', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In FOLLOW set calculations, look strictly at the RIGHT-HAND SIDE of productions to see what symbols immediately follow non-terminal A!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Computing FIRST and FOLLOW', 'problem': 'Given grammar: S → A a, A → b | ε. Compute FIRST(S) and FOLLOW(A).', 'steps': ['Step 1: FIRST(A) = { b, ε }.', "Step 2: FIRST(S): S → A a. FIRST(A) has 'b', and because A produces ε, 'a' follows ⇒ FIRST(S) = { b, a }.", "Step 3: FOLLOW(A): Look where A appears on RHS: S → A a. What follows A? Terminal 'a'.", 'Step 4: Therefore, FOLLOW(A) = { a }.'], 'answer': 'FIRST(S) = { a, b }, FOLLOW(A) = { a }.'}], 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'GATE Trap: Longest Prefix Match (Maximal Munch)', 'warning': "If the input code is 'int_var', the lexer does NOT match 'int' as a keyword and '_var' as identifier! It reads the longest valid token: 'int_var' as a single identifier."}]}, ('CD', 1): {'sub_id': 'CD', 'chap_idx': 1, 'title': 'Top-Down (LL(1)) & Bottom-Up (LR) Parsing', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Example 1: Lookahead at an Intersection (LL(1) Parser)', 'text': "When driving toward a fork in the road, an LL(1) parser looks exactly 1 token ahead into the future to decide which branch of grammar to take.\nIf two different grammar rules start with the same token, the parser crashes with a conflict! That's why LL(1) grammars must be left-factored and cannot contain left recursion."}], 'foundations': [{'number': '24.1', 'title': 'LL(1) Grammar Conditions', 'content': 'A grammar G is LL(1) if and only if for every production <code>A → α | β</code>:<br/>• 1. <code>FIRST(α) ∩ FIRST(β) = ∅</code> (No common starting terminal).<br/>• 2. At most one of α, β can produce ε.<br/>• 3. If β produces ε, then <code>FIRST(α) ∩ FOLLOW(A) = ∅</code>.<br/><br/>• <b>Grammars that are NEVER LL(1):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Left-recursive grammars (<code>A → A α</code>).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Ambiguous grammars.', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Any grammar with Left Recursion is 100% GUARANTEED NOT to be LL(1)! Always eliminate left recursion before testing LL(1).'}, {'number': '24.2', 'title': 'Hierarchy and Power of LR Parsers', 'content': 'From least powerful to most powerful bottom-up parser:<br/><br/><b>LR(0) ⊂ SLR(1) ⊂ LALR(1) ⊂ CLR(1)</b><br/><br/>• <b>LR(0):</b> Places reduce actions across the entire row without looking ahead.<br/>• <b>SLR(1):</b> Places reduce actions ONLY in the columns of <code>FOLLOW(A)</code>.<br/>• <b>LALR(1):</b> Merges CLR(1) states that have identical LR(0) cores.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Number of states in LALR(1) == Number of states in LR(0) / SLR(1)</b>!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Merging states in LALR(1) can introduce <b>Reduce/Reduce conflicts</b>, but <b>NEVER Shift/Reduce conflicts</b>.<br/>• <b>CLR(1):</b> Most powerful deterministic parser; has the most states.', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: If a grammar is SLR(1), it is GUARANTEED to be LALR(1) and CLR(1)! If a grammar has a Shift-Reduce conflict in LALR(1), that same conflict was ALREADY present in CLR(1).'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Eliminating Left Recursion', 'problem': 'Eliminate immediate left recursion from: E → E + T | T.', 'steps': ["Step 1: Identify pattern: A → A α | β, where A = E, α = '+ T', β = 'T'.", "Step 2: Rule: Replace with A → β A' and A' → α A' | ε.", "Step 3: Write new productions: E → T E' and E' → + T E' | ε."], 'answer': "E → T E', E' → + T E' | ε."}], 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'GATE Trap: Operator Precedence Grammar', 'warning': 'An Operator Grammar can NEVER contain ε-productions, and can NEVER have two non-terminals adjacent to each other on the right-hand side (e.g. A → B C is forbidden)!'}]}, ('CD', 2): {'sub_id': 'CD', 'chap_idx': 2, 'title': 'SDT, Intermediate Code & Optimization', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Example 1: Constant Folding at Compile Time', 'text': 'If your code writes: `int seconds = 60 * 60 * 24;`\nDoes your CPU calculate that multiplication every time your app runs? No! The compiler evaluates 60 × 60 × 24 = 86400 right during compilation and simply replaces the code with `int seconds = 86400;`. This is Constant Folding.'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '25.1', 'title': 'S-Attributed vs L-Attributed SDT', 'content': '• <b>Synthesized Attribute:</b> Computed from the attribute values of children nodes (Bottom-up flow).<br/>• <b>Inherited Attribute:</b> Computed from parents or left siblings (Top-down / Left-to-right flow).<br/><br/>• <b>S-Attributed SDT:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Uses ONLY Synthesized attributes.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Evaluated during bottom-up LR parsing.<br/><br/>• <b>L-Attributed SDT:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Can use Synthesized attributes AND Inherited attributes that depend only on parents and <b>LEFT SIBLINGS</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Every S-Attributed SDT is automatically L-Attributed</b> (S ⊂ L)!', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In an L-attributed SDT, an inherited attribute of a node can depend on its left siblings, but NEVER its right siblings!'}, {'number': '25.2', 'title': 'Common Code Optimizations', 'content': "• <b>Constant Folding:</b> Pre-calculates constant operations at compile time (e.g. <code>2 + 3 → 5</code>).<br/>• <b>Constant Propagation:</b> Replaces variables holding known constants with their values.<br/>• <b>Common Subexpression Elimination:</b> Re-uses already computed expressions.<br/>• <b>Dead Code Elimination:</b> Removes unreachable code or variables whose values are never used.<br/>• <b>Loop Invariant Code Motion:</b> Moves statements that don't change inside a loop to OUTSIDE the loop.", 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG) for Basic Blocks: Leaf nodes represent initial values/identifiers. Interior nodes represent operators. A node with multiple parents represents a shared Common Subexpression!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Basic Block Identification', 'problem': 'How many basic blocks are in a program with: 1. i=1, 2. if i>10 goto 6, 3. x=x+i, 4. i=i+1, 5. goto 2, 6. print x?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Leaders are: (1) First instruction, (2) Target of any jump/branch, (3) Instruction immediately following a jump/branch.', 'Step 2: Line 1 is a leader (first statement).', 'Step 3: Line 2 is a leader (target of goto in line 5).', 'Step 4: Line 3 is a leader (immediately follows conditional branch at line 2).', 'Step 5: Line 6 is a leader (target of jump at line 2).', 'Step 6: Leaders are lines: 1, 2, 3, 6 ⇒ Total 4 Basic Blocks.'], 'answer': 'Total Basic Blocks = 4.'}], 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'GATE Trap: Static Single Assignment (SSA)', 'warning': 'In SSA form, every variable is assigned EXACTLY ONCE. Merging variables from different branches requires Φ (phi) functions.'}]}, ('OS', 0): {'sub_id': 'OS', 'chap_idx': 0, 'title': 'Process Management, CPU Scheduling & Threads', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Example 1: Grocery Store Checkout (CPU Scheduling Algorithms)', 'text': '• FCFS (First-Come First-Served): You wait behind someone with 3 overflowing carts (Convoy Effect: average wait time explodes!).\n• SJF (Shortest Job First): The customer with just 1 packet of gum checks out first (Provably gives the lowest average waiting time!).\n• Round Robin: Each customer gets 30 seconds of cashier scanning before rotating to the next person (Fair, highly responsive, zero starvation).'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '26.1', 'title': 'The fork() System Call & Process Tree', 'content': '• <code>fork()</code> duplicates the calling process to create a child process.<br/>• Return value of <code>fork()</code>:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Returns <b>0</b> to the Child process.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Returns <b>Child PID (> 0)</b> to the Parent process.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Returns <b>negative</b> on failure.<br/><br/>• <b>Number of processes created:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;If <code>fork()</code> is called n times consecutively:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Total processes created = <b>2^n</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Total child processes created = <b>2^n - 1</b>.', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: When fork() is called inside loops or logical checks like fork() && fork(), trace child and parent branches independently: in (fork() && fork()), the second fork() executes ONLY in the parent process because fork() returns 0 (False) to the child!'}, {'number': '26.2', 'title': 'CPU Scheduling Metrics & Formulas', 'content': '• <b>Turnaround Time (TAT):</b> Completion Time - Arrival Time.<br/>• <b>Waiting Time (WT):</b> Turnaround Time - Burst Time.<br/>• <b>Response Time:</b> First Time CPU is Allocated - Arrival Time.<br/><br/>• <b>SJF / SRTF:</b> Provably optimal for minimizing Average Waiting Time.<br/>• <b>Round Robin:</b> Performance depends heavily on Time Quantum (q):<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If q is extremely large: Degenerates into FCFS.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If q is extremely small: High context switch overhead kills CPU efficiency.', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Non-preemptive scheduling algorithms (FCFS, Non-preemptive SJF, Priority) NEVER interrupt a running process until it either completes or yields CPU for I/O!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Calculating Average Waiting Time', 'problem': 'Processes P1, P2, P3 arrive at time 0 with burst times 24, 3, 3 ms under FCFS. Find Average Waiting Time.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Draw Gantt Chart: [ P1: 0 to 24 ] -> [ P2: 24 to 27 ] -> [ P3: 27 to 30 ].', 'Step 2: Waiting Time for P1 = 0 ms.', 'Step 3: Waiting Time for P2 = 24 ms.', 'Step 4: Waiting Time for P3 = 27 ms.', 'Step 5: Average Waiting Time = (0 + 24 + 27) / 3 = 51 / 3 = 17 ms.'], 'answer': 'Average Waiting Time = 17 ms.'}], 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'GATE Trap: User-Level vs Kernel-Level Threads', 'warning': 'If one User-Level Thread (ULT) makes a blocking system call, the ENTIRE PROCESS blocks! Kernel-Level Threads (KLT) allow other threads in the same process to keep running.'}]}, ('OS', 1): {'sub_id': 'OS', 'chap_idx': 1, 'title': 'Process Synchronization, Semaphores & Deadlock Prevention', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Example 1: Coffee Shop Single Restroom Key (Mutual Exclusion)', 'text': 'A coffee shop has 1 restroom and 1 key hanging on the wall (Binary Semaphore = 1).\nWhen someone takes the key (wait / P operation), the key is gone (Semaphore = 0). Anyone else who arrives must wait outside.\nWhen the person returns the key (signal / V operation), the next waiting customer enters. This guarantees Mutual Exclusion.'}, {'title': 'Real-Life Example 2: The Gridlock Traffic Intersection (Deadlock)', 'text': 'Four cars enter a 4-way intersection simultaneously, each blocking the road behind them while waiting for the car ahead to move.\nNobody can move, and nobody will back up. All 4 conditions for Deadlock are present: Mutual Exclusion, Hold & Wait, No Preemption, and Circular Wait.'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '27.1', 'title': 'Critical Section Requirements & Semaphores', 'content': 'Any valid solution to the Critical Section problem MUST satisfy:<br/>• <b>1. Mutual Exclusion (Mandatory):</b> Only 1 process in critical section at a time.<br/>• <b>2. Progress (Mandatory):</b> If no process is in critical section, only processes wanting to enter can participate in the decision.<br/>• <b>3. Bounded Waiting (Mandatory):</b> A limit must exist on the number of times other processes can enter before a request is granted.<br/><br/>• <b>Counting Semaphore:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <code>wait(S)</code> (or P): <code>S = S - 1</code>. If <code>S < 0</code>, process blocks.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <code>signal(S)</code> (or V): <code>S = S + 1</code>. If <code>S <= 0</code>, wakes up a blocked process.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Magnitude <code>|S|</code> when S < 0 gives the exact number of blocked processes waiting in the queue!', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: If a counting semaphore is initialized to 10, and 6 wait() and 4 signal() operations are executed: Final S = 10 - 6 + 4 = 8.'}, {'number': '27.2', 'title': "Deadlock: 4 Conditions & Banker's Algorithm", 'content': "Deadlock occurs if and only if ALL FOUR conditions hold simultaneously:<br/>1. Mutual Exclusion, 2. Hold and Wait, 3. No Preemption, 4. <b>Circular Wait</b>.<br/><br/>• <b>Deadlock Prevention:</b> Invalidate at least one of the 4 conditions (e.g. impose total ordering on resources to eliminate Circular Wait).<br/><br/>• <b>Banker's Algorithm (Deadlock Avoidance):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <code>Need Matrix = Max Demand - Currently Allocated</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- A state is SAFE if there exists at least one sequence of processes that can finish without causing deadlock.", 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: For m instances of a single resource shared by n processes, where each process requires at most k instances: The system is GUARANTEED to be deadlock-free if: Total Resources m ≥ n × (k - 1) + 1!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Minimum Resources to Prevent Deadlock', 'problem': 'A system has 4 processes, each needing a maximum of 3 tape drives. What is the minimum number of tape drives required to guarantee that deadlock will never occur?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Formula for deadlock-free condition: R ≥ Σ(Max_i - 1) + 1.', 'Step 2: Here n = 4 processes, each needing k = 3 units.', 'Step 3: In the worst-case deadlock scenario, each process holds (k - 1) = (3 - 1) = 2 units.', 'Step 4: Total held in worst case = 4 × 2 = 8 tape drives.', 'Step 5: Adding just 1 more unit allows at least one process to finish, release its resources, and free the system.', 'Step 6: Minimum tape drives R = 8 + 1 = 9.'], 'answer': 'Minimum tape drives needed = 9.'}], 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'GATE Trap: Safe State vs Deadlock', 'warning': 'A Safe state is NEVER deadlocked. An Unsafe state is NOT necessarily deadlocked; an unsafe state merely holds the potential to lead into a deadlock if worst-case requests occur!'}]}, ('OS', 2): {'sub_id': 'OS', 'chap_idx': 2, 'title': 'Paging, Virtual Memory, TLB & Inodes', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Example 1: The College Library Desk (Virtual Memory & Paging)', 'text': "A student desk (Physical RAM) has room for only 3 open books (Page Frames).\nYour research paper requires 100 books (Virtual Memory: 4 GB program on a 512 MB laptop!).\nWhen you need chapter 5, you walk to the library shelf (Hard Disk), place it on the desk, and swap out the book you haven't touched for the longest time (LRU Page Replacement)."}], 'foundations': [{'number': '28.1', 'title': 'Paging & Effective Memory Access Time (EMAT) with TLB', 'content': '• <b>Paging Address Structure:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Logical Address = [ Page Number (p) | Page Offset (d) ].<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Page Offset bits = log2(Page Size in bytes).<br/><br/>• <b>Translation Lookaside Buffer (TLB):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;High-speed cache that stores recent Page-to-Frame mappings.<br/><br/>• <b>EMAT Formula with TLB:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>EMAT = Hit_Ratio × (TLB_time + RAM_time) + (1 - Hit_Ratio) × (TLB_time + 2 × RAM_time)</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;(On a TLB miss, we must access RAM twice: first to read the page table, second to read actual data).', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: For a multi-level page table with k levels, a TLB miss requires (k + 1) memory accesses: k accesses to traverse the page table tree, plus 1 access to read data!'}, {'number': '28.2', 'title': 'Page Replacement Algorithms', 'content': "• <b>FIFO (First-In First-Out):</b> Suffers from <b>Belady's Anomaly</b> (allocating MORE page frames can sometimes cause MORE page faults!).<br/>• <b>Optimal Page Replacement (OPT):</b> Replaces page that will not be used for longest time in the future. Provably minimum page faults (used as benchmark).<br/>• <b>LRU (Least Recently Used):</b> Replaces page that hasn't been accessed for longest time in the past. Free from Belady's anomaly (Stack algorithm).", 'crucial_rule': "GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Stack algorithms (like LRU and Optimal) NEVER suffer from Belady's Anomaly because the set of pages in an n-frame memory is always a subset of pages in an (n+1)-frame memory!"}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Calculating EMAT with TLB', 'problem': 'TLB search time = 20 ns, Main Memory access time = 100 ns. TLB hit ratio = 90%. Single-level paging. Find EMAT.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Case 1 (TLB Hit): Access TLB (20 ns) + Access RAM for data (100 ns) = 120 ns. Occurs 90% of the time.', 'Step 2: Case 2 (TLB Miss): Access TLB (20 ns) + Access RAM for Page Table (100 ns) + Access RAM for data (100 ns) = 220 ns. Occurs 10% of the time.', 'Step 3: EMAT = (0.90 × 120 ns) + (0.10 × 220 ns) = 108 ns + 22 ns = 130 ns.'], 'answer': 'Effective Memory Access Time = 130 ns.'}], 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'GATE Trap: Thrashing in Virtual Memory', 'warning': 'Thrashing occurs when total memory demand of active processes exceeds available physical frames. The CPU spends almost 100% of its time swapping pages in/out rather than executing instructions!'}]}, ('DB', 0): {'sub_id': 'DB', 'chap_idx': 0, 'title': 'ER-Model, Relational Algebra & SQL', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Example 1: Hospital Blueprints vs Real Rooms (ER to Relational Tables)', 'text': "Before constructing a hospital, you sketch an architect's blueprint of Doctors, Patients, and Wards (ER Diagram).\nWhen building it, you pour real concrete rooms with ID numbers (Relational Tables).\nBecause one Doctor treats many Patients (1:N relationship), you don't build a separate building: you simply write the Doctor's ID on each Patient's medical chart (Foreign Key placed on the N-side!)."}, {'title': 'Real-Life Example 2: Slicing Vegetables (Selection σ vs Projection π)', 'text': "• Selection (σ): Filtering rows. Like picking only the red tomatoes from a vegetable basket and ignoring the potatoes (filters horizontally).\n• Projection (π): Slicing columns. Like taking a photo of a person's face only and cropping out their clothes and shoes (slices vertically)."}, {'title': 'Real-Life Example 3: The Missing Exam Mark (SQL NULL Behaviour)', 'text': "In SQL, `NULL` does NOT mean zero, and it does NOT mean empty text. It means 'UNKNOWN / MISSING'.\nIf a student's mark is NULL, is their mark greater than 50? The answer is UNKNOWN!\nIn SQL three-valued logic, `NULL = NULL` is NOT True—it is UNKNOWN! That is why writing `WHERE mark = NULL` returns 0 rows; you must write `WHERE mark IS NULL`."}], 'foundations': [{'number': '29.1', 'title': 'ER to Relational Table Conversion Rules', 'content': "How to convert ER relationships into minimal relational tables:<br/><br/>• <b>1:1 Relationship:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Combine into 2 tables by placing the primary key of either entity as a foreign key in the other.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If both entities have Total Participation, they can be merged into a <b>single table</b>.<br/><br/>• <b>1:N Relationship:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Combine into 2 tables.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- The Foreign Key <b>ALWAYS goes to the 'N' (Many) side table</b>!<br/><br/>• <b>M:N Relationship:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- CANNOT be merged into either entity table.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Strictly requires a <b>separate relationship table</b> containing the primary keys of both participating entities.<br/><br/>• <b>Weak Entity:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Always merged with its identifying relationship into 1 table.", 'crucial_rule': "GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In a 1:N relationship, NEVER put the foreign key on the '1' side! It must go to the 'N' side to prevent duplicate rows or multi-valued attributes."}, {'number': '29.2', 'title': 'Relational Algebra vs SQL Rules', 'content': '• <b>Duplicate Elimination:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Relational Algebra automatically eliminates duplicates (Set Semantics).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- SQL keeps duplicates (Bag/Multiset Semantics) unless you write <code>DISTINCT</code>.<br/><br/>• <b>Aggregate Functions (SUM, AVG, MIN, MAX):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- All aggregate functions <b>completely IGNORE NULL values</b>!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Exception: <code>COUNT(*)</code> counts all rows, including rows containing NULLs.<br/><br/>• <b>WHERE vs HAVING:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <code>WHERE</code> filters individual rows BEFORE grouping.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <code>HAVING</code> filters aggregated groups AFTER <code>GROUP BY</code>.', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Writing WHERE AVG(salary) > 50000 causes an SQL SYNTAX ERROR! Aggregate conditions MUST be placed inside the HAVING clause, never WHERE.'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Finding Minimal Tables', 'problem': 'An ER diagram has 3 entities E1, E2, E3. E1 has a 1:N relationship with E2, and E2 has an M:N relationship with E3. No weak entities. What is the minimum number of relational tables needed?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Entity E1 gets Table E1.', "Step 2: 1:N relationship between E1 and E2 is merged into Table E2 (with E1's primary key as foreign key).", 'Step 3: Entity E3 gets Table E3.', 'Step 4: M:N relationship between E2 and E3 CANNOT be merged; it requires a separate Table R.', 'Step 5: Total tables = Table E1 + Table E2 + Table E3 + Table R = 4 tables.'], 'answer': 'Minimum relational tables = 4.'}], 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'GATE Trap: NOT IN with NULL values', 'warning': 'If a subquery returns even one NULL value, `WHERE x NOT IN (subquery)` returns EMPTY SET (zero rows)! Because `x != NULL` evaluates to UNKNOWN, making the whole condition fail. Always use `NOT EXISTS` instead!'}]}, ('DB', 1): {'sub_id': 'DB', 'chap_idx': 1, 'title': 'Functional Dependencies, Normalization & B+ Trees', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Example 1: Updating Your Home Address (Why Normalization Matters)', 'text': 'Imagine a messy college spreadsheet where your home address is typed next to every single course you take.\nIf you enroll in 6 courses, your address is repeated 6 times (Redundancy!). If you move house, the registrar must update 6 rows (Update Anomaly). If they miss one, your records contradict each other.\nNormalization separates Student Address into its own clean table so it is stored in exactly ONE place.'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '30.1', 'title': 'The Step-by-Step Normal Forms Checklist', 'content': 'For every Functional Dependency <b>X → Y</b> in relation R:<br/><br/>• <b>1NF:</b> All attribute values must be atomic (no multi-valued sets or arrays).<br/><br/>• <b>2NF:</b> Relation is in 1NF AND contains <b>NO PARTIAL DEPENDENCIES</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- (No proper subset of Candidate Key can determine a non-prime attribute).<br/><br/>• <b>3NF:</b> Relation is in 2NF AND for every X → Y:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Either <b>X is a Superkey</b>, OR <b>Y is a Prime Attribute</b> (part of some Candidate Key).<br/><br/>• <b>BCNF (Boyce-Codd):</b> Strictly requires that for every X → Y:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>X MUST BE A SUPERKEY</b>!', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Every relation with only 2 attributes is AUTOMATICALLY in BCNF! A decomposition into 3NF is always guaranteed to be both Lossless Join AND Dependency Preserving. A decomposition into BCNF is always Lossless Join, but might NOT preserve dependencies.'}, {'number': '30.2', 'title': 'B-Tree vs B+ Tree Core Properties', 'content': 'In a B+ Tree of order p (maximum pointer capacity):<br/>• <b>Internal Nodes:</b> Store only search keys and child pointers (no data records!). Can hold up to <code>p</code> pointers and <code>p - 1</code> keys.<br/>• <b>Leaf Nodes:</b> Store ALL actual data pointers and keys, linked together as a <b>doubly linked list</b> for fast range queries.<br/><br/>• <b>Node Capacity Conditions:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Root node: At least 2 child pointers (if not leaf).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Internal / Leaf nodes: At least <b>⌈p / 2⌉</b> child pointers / keys.', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: To calculate the order p of a B-tree node fitting in a disk block of B bytes with Key size K and Pointer size P: Equation is: p × P + (p - 1) × K ≤ B!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Step-by-Step Solved Problem: B+ Tree Node Order', 'problem': 'Block size = 512 bytes, Key size = 8 bytes, Block Pointer size = 4 bytes. Find the maximum order p of an internal node.', 'steps': ['Step 1: In an internal node of order p, there are p block pointers and (p - 1) keys.', 'Step 2: Total size equation: p × (Block Pointer) + (p - 1) × (Key) ≤ Block Size.', 'Step 3: Substitute values: p × 4 + (p - 1) × 8 ≤ 512.', 'Step 4: 4p + 8p - 8 ≤ 512 ⇒ 12p ≤ 520 ⇒ p ≤ 520 / 12 = 43.33.', 'Step 5: Since order p must be an integer: p = 43.'], 'answer': 'Maximum order p = 43.'}], 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'GATE Trap: Lossless Join Condition', 'warning': 'Decomposition of R into R1 and R2 is Lossless Join if and only if the common attributes (R1 ∩ R2) form a Superkey of AT LEAST ONE of the relations: (R1 ∩ R2) → R1 OR (R1 ∩ R2) → R2!'}]}, ('DB', 2): {'sub_id': 'DB', 'chap_idx': 2, 'title': 'Transactions, Serializability & Two-Phase Locking (2PL)', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Example 1: ATM Cash Withdrawal (ACID Properties)', 'text': '• Atomicity (All or Nothing): If the ATM dispenses cash but the power cuts before debiting your bank balance, the whole transaction rolls back.\n• Consistency: Total money before and after transfer remains constant.\n• Isolation: If your spouse transfers money at the exact same second, transactions run isolated without interfering.\n• Durability: Once the transaction commits, a power blackout cannot erase the record.'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '31.1', 'title': 'Conflict Serializability & Precedence Graphs', 'content': 'Two operations conflict if they: (1) belong to different transactions, (2) access the SAME data item, and (3) <b>AT LEAST ONE IS A WRITE</b>.<br/><br/>• <b>Testing Conflict Serializability:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Draw a directed Precedence Graph with a node for each transaction Ti.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. Draw an edge <code>Ti → Tj</code> if an operation in Ti conflicts with and happens BEFORE an operation in Tj.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>The schedule is Conflict Serializable IF AND ONLY IF the Precedence Graph has NO CYCLES!</b>', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Every Conflict Serializable schedule is automatically View Serializable! But a View Serializable schedule with no blind writes is guaranteed to be Conflict Serializable.'}, {'number': '31.2', 'title': 'Two-Phase Locking (2PL)', 'content': '• <b>Basic 2PL:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Growing Phase: Can acquire locks, CANNOT release locks.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Shrinking Phase: Can release locks, CANNOT acquire new locks.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Guarantees Conflict Serializability, but can STILL SUFFER FROM DEADLOCKS!</b><br/><br/>• <b>Strict 2PL:</b> All exclusive (write) locks held until COMMIT/ABORT. Prevents cascading rollbacks.<br/>• <b>Rigorous 2PL:</b> ALL locks (read and write) held until COMMIT/ABORT.', 'crucial_rule': "GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Basic 2PL is NOT deadlock-free! Two transactions can easily wait on each other's locks in basic 2PL."}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Precedence Graph Cycle Check', 'problem': 'Check if Schedule S: r1(X); r2(Y); w1(Y); w2(X) is Conflict Serializable.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Check pair r2(Y) and w1(Y): Both access Y, one is write. r2 is before w1 ⇒ Edge T2 → T1.', 'Step 2: Check pair r1(X) and w2(X): Both access X, one is write. r1 is before w2 ⇒ Edge T1 → T2.', 'Step 3: Graph has edges T2 → T1 and T1 → T2, forming a DIRECTED CYCLE: T1 ⇆ T2.', 'Step 4: Because there is a cycle, schedule S is NOT conflict serializable.'], 'answer': 'Schedule is NOT Conflict Serializable.'}], 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'GATE Trap: Recoverability vs Cascadelessness', 'warning': 'Recoverable schedule: If Tj reads from Ti, Ti MUST commit BEFORE Tj commits! Cascadeless schedule: If Tj reads from Ti, Ti must commit BEFORE Tj even READS the data!'}]}, ('CN', 0): {'sub_id': 'CN', 'chap_idx': 0, 'title': 'OSI/TCP-IP Layers, Framing & CRC', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Example 1: Sending an International Gift (Layered Architecture)', 'text': 'When sending a gift overseas:\n• Application: You choose the watch gift.\n• Presentation: Gift wrapping.\n• Transport: Tracking number & guarantee of delivery.\n• Network: Postal address with Country and Zip code (Routing).\n• Data Link: Local delivery van driving from warehouse to sorting hub (Hop-by-hop delivery).\n• Physical: The actual road asphalt and tires.'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '32.1', 'title': 'CRC (Cyclic Redundancy Check) Polynomial Division', 'content': 'CRC uses binary modulo-2 arithmetic (XOR without carry):<br/><br/>• 1. If generator polynomial G(x) has degree <b>k</b>, append <b>k zeros</b> to the original data bitstring.<br/>• 2. Perform modulo-2 division of the padded data by G(x).<br/>• 3. The <b>k-bit remainder</b> is the CRC checksum.<br/>• 4. Transmitted frame = <code>Original Data + Remainder</code>.<br/>• 5. Receiver divides received frame by G(x): If remainder is 0, frame is error-free!', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: If a generator polynomial G(x) has degree k, it ALWAYS appends exactly k parity bits! A polynomial detects all odd-bit errors if it has (x + 1) as a factor.'}, {'number': '32.2', 'title': 'Hamming Code Distance & Error Detection/Correction', 'content': '• <b>Hamming Distance (d):</b> Number of bit positions in which two codewords differ.<br/>• To detect <b>t</b> errors: Minimum distance <code>d_min ≥ t + 1</code>.<br/>• To correct <b>t</b> errors: Minimum distance <code>d_min ≥ 2t + 1</code>.', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: To correct single-bit errors (t = 1), d_min must be at least 2(1) + 1 = 3! Parity bit positions in Hamming code are powers of 2: 1, 2, 4, 8, 16...'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Hamming Distance Detection', 'problem': 'A code has minimum Hamming distance d_min = 5. How many bit errors can it detect, and how many can it correct?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Formula for error detection: d_min ≥ t_detect + 1 ⇒ t_detect = d_min - 1.', 'Step 2: Maximum detectable errors = 5 - 1 = 4 errors.', 'Step 3: Formula for error correction: d_min ≥ 2 t_correct + 1 ⇒ 2 t_correct ≤ 5 - 1 = 4.', 'Step 4: Maximum correctable errors = 4 / 2 = 2 errors.'], 'answer': 'Can detect up to 4 errors, can correct up to 2 errors.'}], 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'GATE Trap: Byte Stuffing / Flag Byte', 'warning': 'In byte stuffing, if the escape byte (ESC) or flag byte (FLAG) appears inside payload data, the sender inserts an extra ESC byte immediately before it.'}]}, ('CN', 1): {'sub_id': 'CN', 'chap_idx': 1, 'title': 'Flow Control Protocols & CSMA/CD', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Example 1: Walkie-Talkie vs Phone Call (Stop-and-Wait vs Sliding Window)', 'text': "• Stop-and-Wait: Walkie-talkie. You say one sentence, say 'Over', and wait in dead silence until the other person replies 'Received, Over'. Most of the time is wasted waiting for replies!\n• Sliding Window: Phone call. You speak continuously without stopping, while the listener nods in the background. High efficiency!"}], 'foundations': [{'number': '33.1', 'title': 'Sliding Window Protocol Efficiency Formulas', 'content': 'Let <code>T_t = Packet Size / Bandwidth</code> (Transmission time), and <code>T_p = Distance / Speed</code> (Propagation time).<br/>Define <code>a = T_p / T_t</code>.<br/><br/>• <b>Stop-and-Wait Efficiency (η):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>η = 1 / (1 + 2a)</code>.<br/><br/>• <b>Sliding Window with window size W:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>η = min(1, W / (1 + 2a))</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Optimal window size for 100% efficiency: <b>W_opt = 1 + 2a</b>.<br/><br/>• <b>Window Sizes for n-bit sequence numbers:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Go-Back-N (GBN): Sender Window = <b>2^n - 1</b>, Receiver Window = <b>1</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Selective Repeat (SR): Sender Window = <b>2^(n-1)</b>, Receiver Window = <b>2^(n-1)</b>.', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In Selective Repeat, Sender Window + Receiver Window ≤ 2^n. If sender and receiver share equal window sizes, maximum window size is exactly 2^(n-1)!'}, {'number': '33.2', 'title': 'CSMA/CD Minimum Frame Size', 'content': 'In Ethernet CSMA/CD, a sender must keep transmitting long enough to detect a collision from the farthest end of the cable:<br/>• <b>Transmission Time ≥ 2 × Propagation Time</b>: <code>T_t ≥ 2 × T_p</code>.<br/>• <b>Minimum Frame Length:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>Length_min = 2 × (Distance / Signal Speed) × Bandwidth</code>.', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: If bandwidth doubles, the minimum frame size MUST DOUBLE to keep collision detection working over the same cable length!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Minimum Frame Size in CSMA/CD', 'problem': 'A 10 Mbps Ethernet has a 1 km cable with signal speed 2 × 10^8 m/s. Find minimum frame size.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Calculate T_p = Distance / Speed = 1000 m / (2 × 10^8 m/s) = 5 × 10^-6 s = 5 μs.', 'Step 2: Condition for CSMA/CD: T_t ≥ 2 × T_p = 2 × 5 μs = 10 μs.', 'Step 3: Minimum Frame Size = Bandwidth × T_t = 10 × 10^6 bps × 10 × 10^-6 s = 100 bits.'], 'answer': 'Minimum frame size = 100 bits.'}], 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'GATE Trap: Go-Back-N Retransmissions', 'warning': 'In GBN, if packet k is lost, ALL subsequent packets in the window (k+1, k+2...) are DISCARDED by the receiver and must be retransmitted by the sender!'}]}, ('CN', 2): {'sub_id': 'CN', 'chap_idx': 2, 'title': 'IPv4, Subnetting, Routing & Fragmentation', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Example 1: Cutting a Huge Birthday Cake (IP Fragmentation)', 'text': "A bakery delivers a 1500 cm long cake, but your small doorway allows boxes of at most 500 cm (MTU: Maximum Transmission Unit).\nYou slice the cake into three 500 cm boxes and attach labels: 'Box 1, Offset 0', 'Box 2, Offset 500', 'Box 3, Offset 1000'.\nIn IPv4, Fragment Offset counts 8-byte chunks so the receiver can reconstruct the packet in the exact right order."}], 'foundations': [{'number': '34.1', 'title': 'IPv4 Subnetting & Usable Hosts', 'content': 'In CIDR notation <code>a.b.c.d / n</code>:<br/>• Network bits = <code>n</code>. Host bits = <code>32 - n</code>.<br/>• Total IP addresses in subnet = <code>2^(32 - n)</code>.<br/>• <b>Usable Host addresses = 2^(32 - n) - 2</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;(Subtract 2: First address is Network ID; Last address is Direct Broadcast ID).', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In a /30 subnet: 2^(32-30) = 4 IPs. Minus 2 = exactly 2 usable host addresses (ideal for point-to-point router links)!'}, {'number': '34.2', 'title': 'Fragmentation Offset Calculation Rules', 'content': '• <b>Fragment Offset:</b> Offset field in IPv4 header is measured in units of <b>8 bytes</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>Offset = (Byte offset of data) / 8</code>.<br/>• <b>Data payload in each fragment (except last) MUST be a multiple of 8 bytes!</b><br/>• <b>MF (More Fragments) Flag:</b> 1 for all fragments EXCEPT the last fragment (which has MF = 0).', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: IP header size (typically 20 bytes) is subtracted from MTU first! If MTU = 1500 bytes: Max data per fragment = 1500 - 20 = 1480 bytes (which is a multiple of 8: 1480 / 8 = 185).'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Subnet Mask Calculation', 'problem': 'An organization needs 4 subnets, each supporting up to 50 hosts. Find the smallest CIDR subnet mask.', 'steps': ['Step 1: For 50 hosts, find host bits h: 2^h - 2 ≥ 50 ⇒ 2^h ≥ 52 ⇒ h = 6 bits (2^6 - 2 = 62 hosts).', 'Step 2: Subnet prefix length n = 32 - h = 32 - 6 = 26 bits.', 'Step 3: Subnet mask in slash notation = /26 (in dotted decimal: 255.255.255.192).'], 'answer': 'Subnet Mask = /26 (255.255.255.192).'}], 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'GATE Trap: Count to Infinity Problem', 'warning': 'Distance Vector routing (RIP) uses Bellman-Ford and suffers from the Count-to-Infinity problem when a link breaks. Solved by Split Horizon and Poison Reverse.'}]}, ('CN', 3): {'sub_id': 'CN', 'chap_idx': 3, 'title': 'TCP Congestion Control & Application Protocols', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Example 1: Testing Ice on a Frozen Pond (TCP Congestion Control)', 'text': '• Slow Start: You step on the ice, take 1 step, then 2 steps, then 4 steps (doubling exponentially) to see if it holds.\n• Congestion Avoidance: Once you pass threshold (ssthresh), you slow down and advance carefully by 1 step per round (linear additive increase).\n• Multiplicative Decrease: If the ice cracks (packet loss), you panic and immediately cut your speed by half or reset to 1!'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '35.1', 'title': 'TCP Congestion Window Phases', 'content': '• <b>1. Slow Start Phase (cwnd < ssthresh):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Window grows <b>EXPONENTIALLY</b>: Doubles every RTT (<code>cwnd = cwnd * 2</code>).<br/><br/>• <b>2. Congestion Avoidance Phase (cwnd ≥ ssthresh):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Window grows <b>LINEARLY</b>: Increases by 1 MSS every RTT (Additive Increase).<br/><br/>• <b>3. On Congestion Event:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Timeout (severe congestion):</b> <code>ssthresh = cwnd / 2</code>, and <code>cwnd is RESET to 1 MSS</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>3 Duplicate ACKs (mild congestion / Fast Recovery):</b> <code>ssthresh = cwnd / 2</code>, and <code>cwnd = ssthresh</code> (continues linear growth).', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: If cwnd is 32 KB and a TIMEOUT occurs: ssthresh becomes 32/2 = 16 KB, and cwnd resets to 1 KB! If 3 duplicate ACKs occur: cwnd drops to 16 KB directly without resetting to 1.'}, {'number': '35.2', 'title': 'Application Layer Protocols Summary', 'content': '• <b>DNS:</b> Port 53, uses <b>UDP</b> for fast queries; uses TCP for zone transfers.<br/>• <b>HTTP:</b> Port 80, TCP-based. Persistent HTTP/1.1 uses 1 RTT per connection.<br/>• <b>SMTP:</b> Port 25, TCP-based push protocol for sending emails.<br/>• <b>POP3 / IMAP:</b> Ports 110 / 143, TCP-based pull protocols for retrieving emails.<br/>• <b>FTP:</b> Uses 2 separate TCP connections: <b>Port 21 for Control</b>, <b>Port 20 for Data</b>.', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: FTP is an Out-of-Band protocol because it transmits commands on Port 21 and payload data on a completely separate connection on Port 20!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Tracing TCP Window Growth', 'problem': 'TCP starts with cwnd = 1 MSS, ssthresh = 8 MSS. How many RTTs are required for cwnd to reach 12 MSS if no packets are lost?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Start RTT 0: cwnd = 1 MSS (Slow Start).', 'Step 2: RTT 1: cwnd = 2 MSS.', 'Step 3: RTT 2: cwnd = 4 MSS.', 'Step 4: RTT 3: cwnd = 8 MSS (Hits ssthresh = 8! Switches to Congestion Avoidance / linear growth).', 'Step 5: RTT 4: cwnd = 8 + 1 = 9 MSS.', 'Step 6: RTT 5: cwnd = 10 MSS.', 'Step 7: RTT 6: cwnd = 11 MSS.', 'Step 8: RTT 7: cwnd = 12 MSS.'], 'answer': '7 RTTs are required.'}], 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'GATE Trap: TCP Header Size', 'warning': 'The minimum TCP header size is 20 bytes (when Options field is empty). The 4-bit Data Offset (header length) field is multiplied by 4 bytes, so maximum header size is 15 × 4 = 60 bytes!'}]}, ('GA', 0): {'sub_id': 'GA', 'chap_idx': 0, 'title': 'Quantitative Aptitude - Work, Speed, Ratios & Percentages', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': "Real-Life Example 1: The '50% + 50% Off' Diwali Sale Trick", 'text': "A store advertises 'Flat 50% + 50% Discount'. Is the item completely free (100% off)? Of course not!\nIf a shirt costs ₹1000, first 50% off cuts it to ₹500. The second 50% off takes half of ₹500 = ₹250.\nTotal discount is 75%, NOT 100%! Formula: Net Discount = x + y - (xy / 100)."}], 'foundations': [{'number': '36.1', 'title': 'Time, Speed, Distance & Relative Speed', 'content': '• <b>Unit Conversion:</b> Multiply km/h by <b>5 / 18</b> to convert to m/s.<br/><br/>• <b>Relative Speed:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Opposite directions (moving towards each other): Relative Speed = <b>S1 + S2</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Same direction (chasing): Relative Speed = <b>|S1 - S2|</b>.<br/><br/>• <b>Average Speed for equal distance covered at speeds S1 and S2:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Average Speed = <b>(2 × S1 × S2) / (S1 + S2)</b> (Harmonic Mean, NOT arithmetic average!).', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: If a person travels to college at 60 km/h and returns at 40 km/h, the average speed is NOT 50 km/h! It is 2(60)(40)/(60+40) = 4800/100 = 48 km/h!'}, {'number': '36.2', 'title': 'Time & Work (The 1-Day Work Method)', 'content': "• If Person A completes a work in D days, A's 1-day work = <b>1 / D</b>.<br/>• If A and B work together: Combined 1-day work = <code>(1 / D_A) + (1 / D_B)</code>.<br/>• <b>Total days needed together:</b> <code>(D_A × D_B) / (D_A + D_B)</code>.", 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: You can also solve Time & Work by taking the LCM of individual days as total work units, turning fractions into simple integer additions!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Work Together Calculation', 'problem': 'A can finish a task in 12 days, and B can finish it in 24 days. How many days will they take working together?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Take LCM of 12 and 24 = 24 total units of work.', "Step 2: A's efficiency = 24 / 12 = 2 units/day.", "Step 3: B's efficiency = 24 / 24 = 1 unit/day.", 'Step 4: Combined efficiency = 2 + 1 = 3 units/day.', 'Step 5: Total days = 24 units / 3 units/day = 8 days.'], 'answer': 'Time taken together = 8 days.'}], 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'GATE Trap: Successive Percentage Changes', 'warning': 'If a salary increases by 20% and then decreases by 20%, the net result is NOT zero! Formula: x + y + xy/100 = +20 - 20 - (20×20/100) = -4% net loss.'}]}, ('GA', 1): {'sub_id': 'GA', 'chap_idx': 1, 'title': 'Analytical & Spatial Reasoning - Blood Relations, Clocks & Cubes', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Example 1: Folding a Cardboard Box (Spatial Reasoning)', 'text': 'When you unfold a cubic cardboard box into a 2D cross pattern, two faces that are separated by one intervening face can NEVER touch: they will become opposite faces when folded back up into a 3D cube.'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '37.1', 'title': 'Clock Angle Formula', 'content': 'The angle θ between the Hour hand and Minute hand at H hours and M minutes is:<br/>• <b>θ = | 30 H - (11/2) M |</b>.<br/>• Speed of minute hand = 6° per minute.<br/>• Speed of hour hand = 0.5° per minute.', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: If the calculated angle θ > 180°, subtract from 360° to find the smaller interior angle between the hands!'}, {'number': '37.2', 'title': 'Painted Cube Slicing Formulas', 'content': 'A large cube painted on all sides is cut into n × n × n small unit cubes:<br/>• <b>3 faces painted (Corners):</b> ALWAYS <b>8 cubes</b> (for any n ≥ 2).<br/>• <b>2 faces painted (Edges):</b> <b>12 × (n - 2)</b>.<br/>• <b>1 face painted (Surfaces):</b> <b>6 × (n - 2)^2</b>.<br/>• <b>0 faces painted (Interior core):</b> <b>(n - 2)^3</b>.', 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Notice the multipliers 8, 12, 6, 1 correspond directly to the geometry of a cube: 8 corners, 12 edges, 6 faces, 1 core!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Angle Between Clock Hands', 'problem': 'Find the angle between hour hand and minute hand at 3:30.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Use formula: θ = | 30 H - 5.5 M | with H = 3, M = 30.', 'Step 2: 30 × 3 = 90.', 'Step 3: 5.5 × 30 = 165.', 'Step 4: θ = | 90 - 165 | = |-75| = 75°.'], 'answer': 'Angle at 3:30 = 75°.'}], 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'GATE Trap: Leap Year Exception', 'warning': 'Century years are leap years ONLY if divisible by 400! For example, the year 2000 was a leap year, but 1900 was NOT a leap year.'}]}, ('GA', 2): {'sub_id': 'GA', 'chap_idx': 2, 'title': 'Verbal Aptitude - Grammar, Concord & Critical Reasoning', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Example 1: The Team Captain Dilemma (Subject-Verb Agreement)', 'text': "Look at this sentence: 'The captain, along with all of his players, (is / are) arriving now.'\nMost students see 'players' (plural) and pick 'are'. That is INCORRECT!\nPhrases like 'along with', 'as well as', 'together with' are merely parenthetical descriptions. The true grammatical subject is 'The captain' (singular), so the correct verb is 'is'!"}], 'foundations': [{'number': '38.1', 'title': 'Subject-Verb Agreement (Concord) Rules', 'content': "• <b>Singular Indefinite Pronouns:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Words like <i>Everyone, Everybody, Neither, Either, Each, None</i> take a <b>SINGULAR VERB</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Correct: 'Each of the students <b>is</b> ready' (NOT 'are ready').<br/><br/>• <b>Either... Or / Neither... Nor:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;The verb agrees with the <b>SUBJECT CLOSEST TO IT</b>!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- 'Neither the teacher nor the students <b>were</b> present.'<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- 'Neither the students nor the teacher <b>was</b> present.'", 'crucial_rule': "GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In 'Neither A nor B', the verb always matches B (the closest subject)!"}, {'number': '38.2', 'title': 'Common Word Confusions in GATE', 'content': "• <b>Affect vs Effect:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <i>Affect</i> is usually a VERB (to influence: 'Smoking will affect your health').<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <i>Effect</i> is usually a NOUN (a result: 'The effect was immediate').<br/><br/>• <b>Principal vs Principle:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <i>Principal:</i> College head / primary sum of money.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <i>Principle:</i> A fundamental rule or moral value.", 'crucial_rule': 'GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In reading comprehension, never assume facts outside the passage! An inference is valid only if it is strictly entailed by the explicit sentences in the text.'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Step-by-Step Solved Problem: Grammar Correction', 'problem': "Choose correct option: 'A bouquet of yellow roses (lend / lends) color to the room.'", 'steps': ['Step 1: Identify the true grammatical subject.', "Step 2: 'of yellow roses' is a prepositional phrase modifying the noun.", "Step 3: The actual subject is 'A bouquet' (singular).", "Step 4: Singular subject requires a singular verb with 's': 'lends'."], 'answer': "Correct: 'A bouquet of yellow roses lends color to the room.'"}], 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'GATE Trap: Dangling Modifiers', 'warning': "In 'Walking into the room, the smoke was choking': The introductory participle 'Walking' accidentally modifies 'the smoke' (as if smoke was walking!). Correct: 'Walking into the room, I found the smoke choking.'"}]}}
